      "headline": "Carly Rae Jepsen's viral hit proves what fans knew all along",
      "dek": "The Call Me Maybe star returns to the UK Top 40 as her single Run Away With Me gets a GTA VI boost.",
      "date": "November 28, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq3dgp88p744o",
      "body": [
        "Eleven years after it first came out, Carly Rae Jepsen's “Run Away With Me” has entered the UK Top 40 for the first time, carried there by an appearance in Grand Theft Auto VI, and by fans who have insisted all along that it was one of the great pop songs of the last decade.",
        "The track enters the Official Singles Chart at number 28, surpassing the number 58 peak it managed when it was first released in 2015. It is the week's most talked-about chart story, and a long-overdue moment for a song that has spent a decade as a cult favorite rather than a hit.",
        "The turnaround came overnight. After the song appeared in the newly released game, its daily streams were reported to have risen from around 20,000 to 281,000, and on Friday it re-entered the UK chart at its new peak. On November 24, 2026, the song was re-issued, as the surge in streaming turned a catalogue track into a current chart contender.",
        "The placement itself is the kind of scene songs are written for. “Run Away With Me” soundtracks a pivotal sequence in the game's first chapter, where the protagonists get their first taste of the high life, speeding around on jet skis and shooting champagne bottles from the back of a yacht. It is a needle-drop moment: the music does the storytelling, and millions of players heard it at the exact moment the game wanted them to feel invincible.",
        "For Jepsen's fans, the chart position is vindication rather than surprise. Released in July 2015 as the fourth single from her third studio album, Emotion, the song was written with the Swedish production duo Mattman & Robin, Shellback and singer-songwriter Noonie Bao, and accompanied by a do-it-yourself music video filmed by Jepsen's then-partner in Paris, Tokyo and New York City. It never repeated the huge commercial success of “Call Me Maybe”, reaching only the top 30 in the Czech Republic, Scotland and Slovakia, and briefly charting inside the top 100 in the UK, Ireland, Canada and Australia.",
        "But critics loved it from the start. Time magazine described it as “Teenage Dream 2.0”, praising a track that “marries euphoric Swedish dance-pop with a pressing nostalgia for your teenage years”. It has since been ranked among the best songs of 2015 and of the 2010s, and it has long been a highlight of Jepsen's live shows, the song her audiences already knew word for word, even when the charts did not.",
        "The Jepsen revival is only part of a much bigger chart story. Grand Theft Auto VI, released last week, has sent its soundtrack surging through the Official Singles Chart. Justice, Tame Impala and SZA's “Flower In A Hurricane” climbs six places to a new peak of number 3, Dua Lipa's specially recorded “Go Easy” is the week's highest new entry at 21, and Travis Scott scores a Top 40 entry with “Burn This S**t Down” at 39. Further cuts from the game fill the Top 100, including Justice and Michael McDonald's “Starting From Zero” at 44, Tove Lo's “Blood In Your Eyes” at 47, Morgan Wallen's “Last Thing You Need” at 64 and Lana Del Rey's “Crazy For You” at 69.",
        "It is the latest example of a screen placement reviving a decade-old song. The pattern was set when Kate Bush's “Running Up That Hill” returned to the charts after featuring in Stranger Things, and it has repeated with older tracks finding new audiences through films, television and now video games. What is different here is the scale: a single game release has put more than half a dozen songs into the UK Top 100 in the same week.",
        "The wider lesson, for the music industry at least, is about discovery. A song that stalled at number 58 in 2015 can reach number 28 in 2026 without a new recording, a new video or a promotional campaign, simply because millions of people heard it in the right scene at the right moment. Streaming has made the charts a place where old songs compete with new ones on equal terms, and where a cult classic can finally become a hit eleven years late.",
        "For Jepsen, who has spent the years since Emotion building one of pop's most devoted followings, the moment lands as proof of something her fans have said for years: the song was never the problem. It just needed the right stage. Grand Theft Auto VI gave it one, and the chart, at number 28 and climbing, has finally caught up.",
        "Sources: Official Charts, BBC and Wikipedia."
      ],
