      "headline": "Polls open in Taiwan's closely watched local elections",
      "dek": "Polls have opened for local elections in Taiwan. While campaigners have stayed away from the topic of China and focused on issues like transport and childcare, Beijing has repeatedly weighed in.",
      "date": "November 28, 2026",
      "source": "DW",
      "source_url": "https://www.dw.com/en/polls-open-in-taiwan-s-closely-watched-local-elections/a-80083086",
      "body": [
        "Taiwan went to the polls on Saturday, November 28, in local elections that will decide control of its cities and counties, and, more importantly, will be read as the first nationwide verdict on President Lai Ching-te’s government since his ruling party’s attempt to recall opposition lawmakers collapsed earlier this year.",
        "Polling stations opened at 8am and were due to close at 4pm, with results expected in the evening. Some 19.65 million people were eligible to vote for 11,051 posts across 22 cities and counties, in elections known locally as the “nine-in-one” vote because so many offices are contested at once.",
        "The race that has dominated the campaign is for Taipei, the capital. The incumbent mayor, Chiang Wan-an of the Kuomintang, the main opposition party, is seeking re-election against Puma Shen of the ruling Democratic Progressive Party, a lawmaker who has built his reputation as an expert on China’s information warfare and as the co-founder of Kuma Academy, a civil-defense school that trains Taiwanese citizens in how to respond to a potential Chinese attack.",
        "The contest has been transformed by a remarkable swing in the polls. In May, a survey by the Taiwan Brain Trust, a think tank associated with the ruling party, put Chiang ahead of Shen by 52.9 percent to 29.7 percent. By the middle of November, the same think tank had Shen ahead by 46.3 percent to 42.2 percent. A blackout on publishing polls came into effect on November 18, so the final state of the race is unknown, but the trend, if it held, would make the Taipei result one of the closest in the city’s history.",
        "Shen’s candidacy has attracted attention well beyond Taiwan. Beijing has opened an investigation into him for secession, and China sent warplanes near Taiwan during the campaign in what many analysts read as an attempt to influence the vote. Shen has made the threat from China a central part of his campaign, arguing that Taipei needs a mayor who understands it, but he has also campaigned on the issues that dominate local politics in the city: transport, childcare, the environment, housing and urban renewal.",
        "His campaign has been visibly aimed at younger voters. Under the slogan “Ocean Current,” his team has organized youth events, promised to plant more trees across the city, and pledged faster responses to welfare applications. Chiang, by contrast, has campaigned on his record as mayor, and on the Kuomintang’s traditional strength in local government: the party won 14 of the 22 cities and counties in the last local elections in 2022, against five for the Democratic Progressive Party.",
        "The stakes go well beyond Taipei. These are the first nationwide elections since the Democratic Progressive Party’s campaign to recall opposition lawmakers collapsed in July 2025, and the results will be read as a measure of support for President Lai, who has been in office since May 2024. A strong result for the ruling party would strengthen his hand; a poor one would embolden the opposition in the legislature, where the government does not have a majority.",
        "The wider geopolitical context is never far away. A report published on November 18 by the United States-China Economic and Security Review Commission warned that Beijing would judge whether coercion could move Taiwan towards unification, and the elections are being watched in Washington and Beijing as a barometer of Taiwanese public opinion. China has made no secret of its preference for the Kuomintang, which favours closer ties with the mainland, over the Democratic Progressive Party, which Beijing regards as separatist.",
        "Analysts say the outcome may come down to turnout, and in particular to whether young and undecided voters, who tend to favor the ruling party, turn out in large numbers. Liu Jia-wei, an assistant professor of political science at National Taipei University, said the result would depend on how many of those voters actually cast a ballot, and on whether the campaign’s focus on local issues, transport, housing, childcare, or on the threat from China proved decisive.",
        "By Saturday evening, Taiwan will know who runs its cities for the next four years. The rest of the world will be watching for something else: a signal about where the island, and its people, stand at a moment when the pressure on them has never been greater.",
        "Sources: Reuters, the Straits Times and reporting on the campaign."
      ],
