      "headline": "US military says it disabled merchant ship trying to breach Iran blockade",
      "dek": "The US military says it disabled the Palau-flagged ship’s propulsion but its crew was unharmed.",
      "date": "November 27, 2026",
      "source": "Al Jazeera",
      "source_url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/27/us-military-says-it-disabled-merchant-ship-trying-to-breach-iran-blockade",
      "body": [
        "The US military said on Friday that it disabled a commercial ship after it attempted to run the American blockade of Iran, in the latest escalation of the confrontation over the Strait of Hormuz. US Central Command said military aircraft launched precision munitions that struck the stern of the Palau-flagged bulk carrier M/V Pacific Archer in the Gulf of Oman on Thursday, after it departed from the Iranian port city of Bandar Abbas. The ship’s propulsion was disabled, while the civilian crew was unharmed.",
        "The strike came after multiple warnings, according to Central Command, which oversees US operations in the Middle East. The Pacific Archer had left Bandar Abbas, an Iranian port on the Strait of Hormuz, and ship-tracking websites list its destination as Singapore. A projectile is shown headed to impact the vessel in footage released by Central Command, in a location given as the Gulf of Oman on November 27, 2026.",
        "The Pacific Archer is at least the 15th disclosed vessel to be disabled by the US military since the blockade began in the spring, and the sixth since it resumed this summer. The previous vessel was disabled on November 7. According to Central Command, US forces have redirected 158 ships and disabled six that refused to comply with warnings not to run the blockade since it resumed, a measure of how systematically the embargo is being enforced, and how often it is being tested.",
        "The United States has been blockading Iranian ports since April, though the blockade was lifted in the summer during talks with Tehran and resumed in July. The war with Iran began at the end of February and has settled into a quiet stalemate in the past several months, with the Trump administration weighing its options in the long-simmering conflict. The blockade is one of the main instruments of that pressure: by preventing Iranian oil and goods from moving by sea, Washington aims to squeeze the Iranian economy and force Tehran to the negotiating table.",
        "Iran’s response has been defiant. An adviser to the Iranian supreme leader, Mohammad Mokhber, said in Tehran on November 27 that the Strait of Hormuz “will certainly not be reopened” and that Iran “will not allow the blockade to remain in place”. The commander of the Iranian navy, Shahram Irani, said on state television that Iran would carry out operations “that will not only break the naval blockade but also drive the enemy out of the region’s waters”.",
        "The confrontation has also produced a war of claims. In a statement posted on X, Central Command denied claims that American forces had seized two Iranian oil tankers, that Iran’s navy had struck the aircraft carrier USS Abraham Lincoln, and that Washington was charging ships $2.5 million to transit the Strait of Hormuz. The denials show how much of the conflict is now fought in public, with both sides using social media to shape the narrative.",
        "The blockade has not been bloodless. Three Indian sailors were killed when American forces struck the Palau-flagged tanker M/T Settebello, officials in India said at the time, a reminder that the crews of the vessels caught in the confrontation are civilians, and that the policy of disabling ships carries real risks for the people on board. In the Pacific Archer case, Central Command stressed that the civilian crew was unharmed.",
        "The scale of the operation is considerable. Al Jazeera reported that the US has spent more than $2 billion operating its naval blockade since July, and has disabled six ships in that period. The cost, and the frequency of the incidents, suggest that the blockade is not a temporary measure but a sustained campaign, and that Iran, for its part, is not backing down.",
        "What happens next will depend on the diplomacy that has so far failed to end the war. Efforts to reach a settlement have stalled, and the Trump administration is weighing its options, including, the president has suggested, renewed military action next year. For now, the pattern is set: Iranian vessels test the blockade, American aircraft disable them, and the Strait of Hormuz remains the most dangerous waterway in the world.",
        "The Pacific Archer is now adrift in the Gulf of Oman, its propulsion disabled, its crew unharmed, its cargo going nowhere. It is the fifteenth ship to be stopped in this way, and it will not be the last."
      ],
