      "section": "us",
      "headline": "Zelensky criticises 'strange' Trump-Putin friendship in BBC interview",
      "dek": "Ukraine's president voices frustration with US and says it is not pushing Russia hard enough in peace talks to end the war.",
      "date": "November 27, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c820q9wgrpyyo",
      "body": [
        "Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky has criticised US President Donald Trump for describing Russian President Vladimir Putin as a friend, calling the friendship strange and saying the Russian leader is an aggressor and a killer who cannot be a friend. His remarks, in an interview with the BBC, are the latest sign of deepening tension between Kyiv and Washington just as peace talks are expected to resume.",
        "Zelensky was speaking in English from his office in Kyiv, in an interview with the BBC published on Thursday and broadcast on Friday. He was asked how he felt when Trump called Putin his friend. Of course it's up to the president of the United States, but it's strange, he replied. He's an aggressor, and a killer. He can't be a friend. Just cannot be. He compared such a friendship to being friends with Adolf Hitler during World War Two. It's the same that somebody would say about Hitler during the Second World War, that he is a good friend. No, he's not a friend.",
        "The remarks were a response to events a week earlier. During a trilateral summit in the Chinese city of Shenzhen on November 19, attended by Trump, Putin and Chinese President Xi Jinping, Trump greeted Putin with a hug and the words: he's my friend. Days later, Trump posted a photograph of himself embracing Putin on his Truth Social platform, referring to the Russian leader as his friend. Zelensky said many people found this deeply strange, and that it was particularly painful for families who had lost their sons and daughters and were pinning their hopes on the US president.",
        "Trump defended his approach, saying his priority was to end the fighting. Asked again about calling Putin a friend, he told reporters: we want to stop killing. I could say he's not a friend. I want to make a deal. The White House has said Trump urged Putin during last week's summit in China to halt military aggression across Europe.",
        "Zelensky's frustration goes beyond the language. He told the BBC that he wants the US to apply more pressure on Putin to stop his invasion of Ukraine, which began almost five years ago and grinds on despite President Donald Trump's efforts to broker a peace deal. He argued that the US was not pushing Russia hard enough in peace talks to end the war, and questioned the US position of maintaining neutrality between the two sides. He said Ukraine needed more progress in peace talks, and that it needed the American position to be an American position, rather than signals from the United States which it could get from Russia. Negotiations are expected to resume in December.",
        "The interview also exposed a practical complaint. Asked by Sarah Rainsford, a BBC correspondent, if he was struggling to protect Ukraine's skies, Zelensky replied: absolutely. It's a huge challenge. It's all about air defense. We have one big problem. We have a deficit with anti-ballistic missiles and systems. The deficit of these missiles, he said, is people's lives. He warned that Ukraine was preparing for a difficult winter as Russian strikes increasingly targeted energy infrastructure, bridges and logistics networks, and complained that the Pentagon was not delivering to Ukraine paid-for Patriot missiles that could stop Russia's ballistic missiles, and that SpaceX's military-grade version of the Starlink satellite internet service, the backbone of Ukraine's drone operations and communication, was not working properly.",
        "Zelensky's comments were broadcast a day after Jared Kushner, Trump's son-in-law and a US envoy trying to mediate an end to the war, complimented Putin on being a grandmaster of politics, and weeks after Kushner's partner in peace efforts, Steve Witkoff, described Putin as a humanitarian. The sequence has underlined how far the tone in Washington has shifted since Trump resumed relations with Moscow.",
        "Europe's support is also under strain. European Union leaders on Thursday failed to reach consensus after fraught talks over a new 49 billion dollar loan for Ukraine's economic and military needs next year. Elections in the EU over the next 12 months could amplify the influence of parties reluctant to send more money to Ukraine and may fray the continent's support, even though Europe has in the past vowed to stay the course in helping Ukraine fend off Russia's invasion.",
        "The legal backdrop adds to the tension. The International Criminal Court has issued an arrest warrant for Putin for war crimes, accusing him of personal responsibility for the abductions of children from Ukraine. The Trump administration, meanwhile, has imposed sweeping sanctions on the court as part of its campaign to dismantle the tribunal over investigations into the US and its allies, including Israel.",
        "Moscow's invasion has killed hundreds of thousands of troops and thousands of Ukrainian civilians. It also brought heavy sanctions on Russia and diplomatically isolated Putin in the West before Trump resumed relations. For Zelensky, the question is whether the country that was once Ukraine's strongest backer will now use its influence to pressure the aggressor, or whether Kyiv will be left to face a difficult winter with a shortage of the missiles that protect its cities."
      ],
