      "section": "war-conflict",
      "headline": "Risks of child marriage in Iran complicated by war",
      "dek": "War, poverty and disrupted schooling could put more girls in Iran at risk of child marriage, rights advocates warn.",
      "date": "November 27, 2026",
      "source": "DW",
      "source_url": "https://www.dw.com/en/risks-of-child-marriage-in-iran-complicated-by-war/a-80075473",
      "body": [
        "War, poverty and disrupted schooling could put more girls in Iran at risk of child marriage, rights advocates are warning, in a country where the law already allows girls under the age of 13 to be married with a guardian’s consent and a court’s approval.",
        "The warning comes as Iran’s economy continues to struggle and as the effects of this year’s war with Israel ripple through everyday life. When families lose income and schools close or are disrupted, campaigners say, the pressure to marry off daughters early grows, and the girls who are married young pay for it for the rest of their lives.",
        "The scale of the problem is hard to pin down, because the Iranian authorities have stopped publishing the numbers. But the figures that are available paint a stark picture. According to Iran’s Statistical Center, 25,900 girls under the age of 15 were married in 2022. Independent researchers believe the real figure is five to six times higher, because so many child marriages are never registered. One estimate puts the number at around 100 underage marriages every day.",
        "A study by the independent researcher Kameel Ahmady, based on fieldwork in seven provinces, found that more than 130,000 girls under 15 were married between 2017 and 2022, and that some 1,500 girls aged between 10 and 14 gave birth during the same period. UNICEF has estimated that 3 percent of children in Iran are married before the age of 15, and 17 percent before the age of 18. Another estimate, from the Women’s News Center of Iran, puts the number of girls under 15 forced into marriage at 131,000 between 2016 and 2021.",
        "The law itself is the starting point. Article 1041 of Iran’s Civil Code allows the marriage of girls under 13, and boys under 15, with the consent of a guardian and the approval of a court. The nominal minimum age for marriage is 18, but judicial exceptions can be granted from the age of 15, and the requirement for court approval is widely ignored in practice, with tens of thousands of marriages taking place outside the courts altogether.",
        "The drivers are, above all, economic. In Ahmady’s research, the most common reason given for marrying a girl early was poverty: in families that cannot afford to feed all their children, marrying off a daughter means one less mouth to feed, and sometimes a bride price that helps the family survive. Patriarchal traditions, isolation and a lack of education reinforce the practice, and it is most common in the country’s poorest provinces, Sistan-Baluchestan, Khuzestan, East Azerbaijan and Kermanshah.",
        "The consequences are well documented. Girls who marry young are far more likely to drop out of school, and far less likely to return to it. Early pregnancy carries serious health risks, and in a country where abortion is heavily restricted, girls who become pregnant at 13 or 14 face dangers that adult women do not. The research also found that early marriage often leads to a cycle of poverty and violence, with girls who marry young more likely to experience domestic abuse and less able to leave it.",
        "The government has made the problem harder to see. The National Statistics Center stopped publishing age breakdowns for marriages in the spring of 2023, and in September 2024 the newspaper Etemad reported that the publication of child-marriage statistics had been halted altogether. Campaigners say the blackout makes it impossible to measure the problem, let alone to address it.",
        "There are also financial incentives that push in the wrong direction. The government offers marriage loans to young couples, and campaigners say that in poor families the loans themselves can become a reason to marry off a daughter early. The loans are not intended to encourage child marriage, but in practice, they argue, they do.",
        "The war has made all of this worse. Schools have been disrupted, incomes have fallen, and families under pressure are more likely to make the decision that campaigners describe as a survival strategy, and that the girls themselves have no say in. The advocates’ warning is that unless the law is changed, and unless the statistics are published again so the problem can be measured, the number of girls married before they have finished school will keep rising, in silence.",
        "What would change the picture, according to the researchers and campaigners, is not mysterious: a clear minimum age of 18 for marriage with no exceptions, enforcement of the law that already exists, support for girls to stay in school, and an end to the statistical blackout. Until then, they say, the girls at risk will remain invisible, and the marriages will go on.",
        "Sources: Kameel Ahmady’s research, Iran Human Rights Monitor, the Women’s News Center of Iran and UNICEF."
      ],
