      "slug": "tui-boss-wants-eu-to-cut-bureaucracy-attract-talent-and",
      "sections": [
        "europe",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "europe",
      "headline": "TUI boss wants EU to cut bureaucracy, attract talent and expand green energy",
      "dek": "Euronews spoke with TUI's CEO Sebastian Ebel about manure-powered cruise ships, migration, dependence on the US, Artificial Intelligence, and the future of tourism.",
      "date": "November 26, 2026",
      "source": "Euronews",
      "source_url": "https://www.euronews.com/2026/11/26/tui-boss-wants-eu-to-cut-bureaucracy-attract-talent-and-expand-green-energy",
      "body": [
        "The head of TUI, the world's largest integrated tourism group, has urged the European Union to cut bureaucracy, attract talented workers and expand green energy, arguing that Europe's competitiveness depends on all three. Sebastian Ebel, the group's chief executive, set out his case in an interview with Euronews at an economic and innovation summit held on a cruise ship in Kiel.",
        "Bureaucracy, Ebel said, is not only the EU's responsibility. National governments are responsible for it as well. He used Denmark as his example: just as much a member of the EU as Germany, he said, but with a far lower density of regulation. That, he argued, was the first point to grasp, that things which can be regulated nationally should be organized nationally, differently and better. The EU apparatus itself, he said, needs to be reformed, streamlined, cut back and focused on guard rails. Ebel, who describes himself as a convinced European, called the EU the best peace model we have ever had, and said reforming it was the way to preserve it.",
        "Cutting bureaucracy is the main point for me, Ebel said. If you work here every day as an entrepreneur, you know what you are talking about, and many people are not aware of the obstacles that exist. That includes taxonomies, he added, meaning a tax system that is simple. It is not always about cuts, he said, but about the system being manageable and understandable. For a company operating across 27 member states, the cost of complexity is paid every day.",
        "Ebel also addressed Europe's demographic challenge. When an age pyramid turns upside down, he said, far fewer people have to support far more older people. That means, mathematically, that people have to work longer, at least those who want to and are able to, and it also means that Europe needs immigration, specifically talented people who can solve the problems the continent faces. He recalled earlier waves of labor migration to Germany, Italians who came to work for Volkswagen in Braunschweig and Wolfsburg, and people from the Philippines who came to work in medical services. The point, he said, is to bring the right people here, who can strongly support Europe in this demographic transition so that it does not backfire.",
        "On energy, Ebel was optimistic about what is possible and blunt about what is failing. An industrial country can be supplied with sustainable energy, he said, because it is the energy produced in the most sustainable and most cost-effective way, and it can be produced even better in the future. Today, he said, Europe is failing because it does not have smart grids, so it cannot deal with different situations, a lot of energy when it is stormy outside, little energy when it is dark and there is no wind, and cannot get that energy to the customer.",
        "He set out three things that needed to happen. First, using the energy available in such a way that when there is a lot of energy it is cheap and when there is little it is expensive, so that self-regulation takes place. Second, expanding the grids, which will increasingly be important. And third, working in large interconnected systems. On nuclear energy, which the EU describes as climate friendly, Ebel expressed doubts about public support for new generations of nuclear plants in countries like Germany.",
        "Ebel also described what TUI itself is doing to decarbonise. The company has committed to electric company cars, is transitioning to electric buses, and favours biogas LNG and e-LNG for its ships, while acknowledging that aviation remains the most challenging area for achieving carbon neutrality. He also stressed the importance of European digital sovereignty, for security and economic reasons, arguing that Europe must not be vulnerable to external decisions or dependent on a single man in America.",
        "Reflecting on 2026, Ebel described it as another difficult year, affected by conflicts in the east, challenges in the west, and the war involving Iran, which caused a sharp slowdown in bookings for two or three months. Despite these disruptions, he said, TUI performed very well and maintained its official financial guidance, demonstrating strong resilience across the tourism group. He predicted continued growth in experiential travel and emphasised the need to offer accessible options for various customer segments.",
        "TUI's scale gives Ebel's views weight. The group, headquartered in Hanover, includes around 460 hotels, 19 cruise ships, five airlines with 125 aircraft and about 1,200 travel agencies, with a total of 67,000 employees. In 2025 it generated revenue of 24.2 billion euros and an operating profit before interest and taxes of 1.46 billion euros. Its annual results for 2026 will be presented on December 9.",
        "Ebel's message, in the end, is that Europe's problems are fixable, but only if it acts. Cut the regulation that does not need to exist, bring in the people the economy needs, and build the grids that would let cheap, clean energy reach the customer. For a tourism company that lives or dies on the health of the European economy, those are not abstract policy debates. They are the conditions on which its business, and the holidays of millions of Europeans, depend."
      ],
