      "slug": "uk-sent-sri-lankan-refugee-to-eswatini-in-secret-deal-the",
      "sections": [
        "europe",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "europe",
      "headline": "UK sent Sri Lankan refugee to Eswatini in secret deal, the BBC says",
      "dek": "The United Kingdom reportedly sent a Sri Lankan refugee who had been convicted of sexual assault to Eswatini, where he committed suicide, as part of a secret deal, according to an investigation by the BBC.",
      "date": "November 26, 2026",
      "source": "Euronews",
      "source_url": "https://www.euronews.com/2026/11/26/uk-sent-sri-lankan-refugee-to-eswatini-in-secret-deal-the-bbc-says",
      "body": [
        "The United Kingdom forcibly resettled a refugee and convicted sex offender in Eswatini, a southern African country with which he had no connection, in a deal that was not disclosed to the UK Parliament or to the local community. The man, a 35-year-old from Sri Lanka with a history of severe mental illness and suicide attempts, took his own life months after arriving in Eswatini. The story was revealed by a BBC investigation published on November 25.",
        "The man was Kishanth Peramalya, a Sri Lankan Tamil who was one of dozens of people rescued by the Royal Navy in the Indian Ocean in October 2021 while trying to sail from India to Canada. He had been granted protection from being returned to Sri Lanka, having been tortured and sexually abused by Sri Lankan military personnel as a teenager, and was later diagnosed with post-traumatic stress disorder. He was not accepted into the UK because he had been convicted of sexual assault offences, and he was held on Diego Garcia, the British Indian Ocean Territory, where he attempted suicide multiple times, according to a UK High Court judgment in February 2025.",
        "The BBC has obtained a copy of the draft deal between the British and Eswatini governments, under which the UK pledged to cover the costs of Peramalya's resettlement for up to three years. The document commits Britain to paying the reasonable costs of his accommodation, healthcare and other support, but it does not specify a total cost, and it does not explain how the two governments would manage the risks he posed to himself and to others, even though it acknowledges that he needed support for his physical and mental health.",
        "Court documents seen by the BBC show that the UK approached more than 20 countries in search of somewhere to send Peramalya, and could not secure an agreement. Then, in February 2026, the United States began using its military base on Diego Garcia in its war with Iran, and the island authorities began withdrawing non-essential personnel. At the end of March, the Maldives offered to take Peramalya for 30 days. He was transferred there before being sent on to Eswatini, formerly known as Swaziland, a country landlocked by South Africa and Mozambique.",
        "Peramalya's family told the BBC that he was initially placed alone in an isolated guesthouse. On September 13, he took his own life. At the time of his death, the British and Eswatini governments had been finalising the agreement for him to live in Eswatini permanently, with the UK to pay for his resettlement. His relatives said they were not aware of his convictions for sexual offences, or of any plan to make his transfer to Eswatini permanent, and they are demanding answers about his treatment.",
        "Migration experts told the BBC that this was the first known case of the UK government forcibly resettling a refugee permanently overseas in such a manner. The case raises questions about how much the UK government should disclose about such arrangements, and how it protects both the people it sends overseas and the communities that receive them. Neither the UK nor the Eswatini authorities answered the BBC's questions on the circumstances surrounding Peramalya's resettlement.",
        "The Eswatini government has pushed back on the BBC's account. Thabile Mdluli, a spokesperson for the government, said the initial arrangement was temporary in nature and intended to safeguard Peramalya's safety while the government of the United Kingdom worked towards a long-term solution regarding his circumstances. She said no formal agreement was ever reached over his long-term residency status with the UK, and declined to comment further on the specific arrangements of his stay in Eswatini or the circumstances of his death. She also said Eswatini had not received any other similar request from the UK government.",
        "The British government, for its part, told the BBC that protecting the British public was its priority, but did not answer questions about the conditions of Peramalya's resettlement. Critics in Eswatini have accused Britain of using the small kingdom as a dumping ground for people it does not want, a charge given weight by a separate fact: Eswatini has accepted dozens of third-country deportees from the United States since July 2025, and in November 2025 its government confirmed it had received 5.1 million dollars in exchange for taking up to 160 migrants from the US.",
        "The case also sits within the longer story of Diego Garcia. In November 2024, the UK allowed 61 migrants to enter from the island after a damning UN report about conditions in the camp there. Peramalya was among those who were not allowed in, because of his convictions, and the search for somewhere to send him became, in effect, a search for a country willing to take a man no other country would accept.",
        "The questions the case leaves behind are now matters for Parliament and for the public. How many similar arrangements exist, and how many more are being negotiated? What duty does a government owe to a person it has recognised as a refugee, even one convicted of serious offences? And what responsibility does it owe to the community in which that person is placed, when the community is not told? Peramalya's family wants answers about his treatment. For now, the only account of what happened to him is the one the BBC obtained, and the draft agreement that neither government has published."
      ],
