      "section": "us",
      "headline": "Extreme weather threatens Thanksgiving festivities in US",
      "dek": "A major storm is forecast to move up the US East Coast and is likely to cause travel delays. Weather warnings were enough to postpone Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade for the first time in its 100 years.",
      "date": "November 26, 2026",
      "source": "DW",
      "source_url": "https://www.dw.com/en/extreme-weather-threatens-thanksgiving-festivities-in-us/a-80055880",
      "body": [
        "For the first time in its 100-year history, New York’s Macy’s Thanksgiving Day Parade has been postponed, the most visible casualty of a powerful storm that swept up the United States East Coast over the Thanksgiving holiday, knocking out power to nearly a million homes, flooding coastal areas and grounding flights on one of the busiest travel weeks of the year.",
        "The parade, which marks its 100th edition this year, was due to step off on Thursday, November 26. Instead, on Wednesday, organizers announced that it would move to Saturday, November 28, starting at 8:30 in the morning. The decision was taken, Macy’s said, “out of an abundance of caution” after the National Weather Service warned of lashing winds and possible coastal flooding in the New York City area, with gusts forecast to reach up to 97 kilometres per hour and rainfall of 2.5 to 5 centimetres from Thursday into Friday.",
        "“While we are heartbroken that our parade was postponed, the safety of our participants and spectators is and always will be our top priority,” Will Coss, Macy’s executive producer, said at a news conference on Wednesday. “We’re saddened by the shift, but we’re optimistic about our opportunity to deliver the most spectacular 100th Macy’s Thanksgiving Day Parade, the best and biggest parade in our history.”",
        "The parade’s history makes the postponement remarkable. Founded in 1924, it was canceled three times during the Second World War, from 1942 to 1944, but it had never before been postponed. The closest it had come was in 1971, when fierce winds forced organizers to keep every giant balloon on the ground while the floats and marching bands carried the show. This year, the balloons were already being inflated on Wednesday when the delay was announced; they were deflated that night, taken to a facility in New Jersey, and were due to be re-inflated on Friday afternoon.",
        "New York City’s mayor, Zohran Mamdani, described the decision as tough but necessary. “I know that this change of plans will cause inconveniences. Yet safety has to come first,” he said. “On Saturday, when balloons climb into the sky, every New Yorker and everyone visiting will be able to take in a show that is both truly spectacular and also safe.” He promised an expanded police presence along the route, and said there were no known specific or credible threats to the parade. Mamdani himself will not attend on Saturday because of a scheduled foot operation.",
        "The parade is a fixture of the American holiday: it typically draws more than 3.5 million spectators along its 2.5-mile route from Manhattan’s Upper West Side to Herald Square, and is broadcast nationwide. This year’s edition was to feature a record 100 balloons, including a five-storey-tall balloon of the cartoon character Bluey wearing a crown and a 17-metre-long Snoopy carrying his bird sidekick Woodstock, as well as a blue hippopotamus, a nod to a balloon that floated away from the 1931 parade and was lost. Singers Raye and Wyclef Jean and actors Leslie Odom Jr and Anthony Ramos were among those scheduled to perform. It remains unclear whether the full line-up will appear on Saturday.",
        "The storm itself was far more than a parade problem. A powerful system brought torrential rain and winds of up to 70 miles per hour along the Eastern Seaboard, toppling trees and power lines and knocking out electricity to nearly a million customers at one point. Heavy rain flooded coastal areas, and flights were delayed across the region during one of the busiest travel weeks of the year. LaGuardia airport reported wind gusts of up to 66 miles per hour and a record 2.18 inches of rain on Thursday, the wettest Thanksgiving on record for Central Park. In Philadelphia, organizers canceled the city’s Thanksgiving Day parade, billed as the oldest in the nation, after first announcing they would ground its balloons.",
        "The disruption extended well beyond the north-east. The National Weather Service issued a coastal flood warning for the San Francisco Bay Area through late Thursday afternoon, warning that flooding could reach 2.7 feet in some low-lying areas as near-record high tides swelled the region’s waterways. Separately, President Donald Trump said on Thursday that he had approved about 100 million dollars in federal aid to Florida following severe storms and flooding there.",
        "Television had to improvise, too. NBC, which airs the parade, said it would broadcast a repeat of the 2025 edition on Thursday instead, with the rescheduled parade airing on Saturday morning. For many Americans, the parade is as much a part of Thanksgiving as the meal itself, and its absence from Thursday’s television schedules was, for millions of households, the clearest sign that this was no ordinary storm.",
        "By Friday, the weather was easing and the focus had shifted to Saturday. The balloons would be back in the sky, the floats would roll, and the 100th parade would finally happen, two days late, but, as its organizers insist, the biggest in its history. The storm that stopped it will be remembered as the one that did what a world war could not: it moved Thanksgiving.",
        "Sources: Al Jazeera, Reuters, CNN and USA Today."
      ],
