      "slug": "can-europe-fix-its-chronic-medicine-shortage-and-supply",
      "sections": [
        "europe",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "europe",
      "headline": "Can Europe fix its chronic medicine shortage and supply chain disruptions?",
      "dek": "The European Parliament just approved new legislation to strengthen the production of essential treatments and cut risks linked to limited suppliers. But will it be enough?",
      "date": "November 25, 2026",
      "source": "Euronews",
      "source_url": "https://www.euronews.com/2026/11/25/can-europe-fix-its-chronic-medicine-shortage-and-supply-chain-disruptions",
      "body": [
        "The European Parliament has given its final approval to a sweeping reform of the European Union's pharmaceutical rules, including a new Critical Medicines Act designed to end the chronic shortages of essential treatments that have hit patients across Europe. The votes, on Wednesday, November 25, conclude more than two years of negotiations over the biggest rewrite of EU medicines law in about two decades.",
        "The Critical Medicines Act, proposed by the European Commission in March 2025, was adopted by 592 votes to 35, with 30 abstentions, after a political agreement between Parliament and the Council in May 2026. It sits alongside the wider pharmaceutical package, a regulation and a directive put forward by the Commission in April 2023 that revise the EU's 20-year-old general pharmaceutical legislation.",
        "At its heart, the Act is about making medicines in Europe again. It sets out criteria for industrial \"strategic projects\" located in the EU to create, modernise and increase manufacturing capacity for critical medicines such as insulin, painkillers and vaccines. Member states will also be able to join forces in voluntary collaborative procurements of five or more countries, and public procurement will be able to favor European production rather than being decided on the lowest price alone.",
        "The new rules also give governments sharper tools against shortages. EU countries will be able to require companies to supply medicines in sufficient quantities to meet patients' needs. Manufacturers must notify national authorities and the European Medicines Agency at least six months in advance if they expect a medicine to be in short supply for more than two weeks, and at least a year in advance if they plan to discontinue a product. The rules cover penicillin-based antibiotics, viral and bacterial vaccines, immunosuppressants, blood thinners and beta blockers, among others.",
        "The shortages the law is meant to fix are real and recent. Shortages of basic drugs reached an all-time high in 2023 and 2024, creating problems for patients and healthcare systems, and they can stem from a sudden increase in demand, a product withdrawal, or a supply chain that depends on a small number of producers, often outside Europe. The Commission's case for the Act was that Europe can no longer afford to depend on third countries for essential medicinal products.",
        "The pharmaceutical package itself focuses on the regulatory side. For newly authorised medicines, a maximum of eleven years of combined regulatory protection is envisaged: eight years of data protection, one additional year of market protection, and the possibility of further periods when certain conditions are met. There are stronger incentives for novel products such as antibiotics, medicines for rare diseases and treatments for children, including a transferable exclusivity voucher for antibiotic developers, and measures to cut red tape and speed access to new treatments.",
        "The Parliament's negotiators presented the votes as a balance between patients and industry. Dolors Montserrat, the EPP group's lead negotiator on the directive, said the package protects \"the investment needed to develop tomorrow's treatments while making Europe a more attractive place for pharmaceutical research and innovation\". Tomislav Sokol, who led negotiations on the Critical Medicines Act for the EPP group, said the Act moves beyond procurement based solely on the lowest price and creates \"strong incentives to bring pharmaceutical manufacturing back to Europe and ensure the security of supply of critical medicines\".",
        "But adoption is not application, and the question in the headline remains open. The Critical Medicines Act still needs formal adoption by the Council, and both texts must be published in the Official Journal before the transition clocks start. Most of the regulation applies 24 months after entry into force, and the shortage rules apply after six months. The cost effects of the exclusivity voucher are uncertain, and the European Medicines Agency only opened a consultation on voucher eligibility on November 25. Whether the new framework actually prevents the next shortage will depend on whether governments enforce the supply duties it creates, and whether the strategic projects it promises are built fast enough to matter."
      ],
