      "slug": "german-hiker-battles-fierce-winds-on-torres-del-paine",
      "sections": [
        "europe",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "europe",
      "headline": "German hiker battles fierce winds on Torres del Paine suspension bridge",
      "dek": "Violent gusts of wind set a suspension bridge swaying in Chile's Torres del Paine National Park. A fellow hiker captures the moment the German tourist struggled to keep his balance.",
      "date": "November 25, 2026",
      "source": "Euronews",
      "source_url": "https://www.euronews.com/2026/11/25/german-hiker-battles-fierce-winds-on-torres-del-paine-suspension-bridge",
      "body": [
        "A German tourist had a frightening encounter with powerful winds on a suspension footbridge in Chile's Torres del Paine National Park, and the video of his struggle has been watched around the world. The footage shows the hiker gripping the bridge's cables with both hands as violent gusts toss the narrow structure from side to side above a deep gorge.",
        "The tourist was identified as Henry Thomas, a 30-year-old from Düsseldorf, who was on a seven-day hiking expedition along the park's O Circuit when the incident occurred on November 11. He was carrying a backpack weighing about 18 kilograms. His partner, Nina, had crossed the bridge moments earlier without incident, when the weather was comparatively calm, so he decided to follow her. Partway across, the wind suddenly picked up.",
        "The bridge is the Puente Guardas, a 52-metre suspension footbridge that spans a gorge roughly 45 metres deep, near the Gray Glacier sector of the park. Footage recorded by fellow hiker Martín Esteban González López shows Thomas spreading his legs to stabilise himself while gripping the side cables, as the walkway swings beneath his feet. Thomas later told Chilean media that he would not have attempted the crossing had the wind been as strong when he first approached the bridge.",
        "The group had been hiking for four days and had just crossed the John Gardner Pass when they came across the suspension bridges. As the wind intensified, Thomas eventually began retreating from the bridge, and the group made its way along an alternative route below the crossing. But the frightening encounter was not over. According to González López, after they had moved away, another powerful gust caused the bridge to twist a full 180 degrees before swinging back into position, rotating like a large pendulum.",
        "A Chilean Weather Service meteorologist estimated winds near 94 kilometres per hour at 600 metres altitude, and said gusts could exceed 150 kilometres per hour because valley slopes can concentrate the flow, although no instruments recorded the wind at the bridge itself. In some areas of the park, gusts are reported to reach speeds of up to 110 kilometres per hour. A park ranger from Chile's National Forestry Corporation (CONAF) arrived at the scene after the incident and stopped additional tourists from crossing, closing the 52-metre bridge. Witnesses said the ranger reached the site roughly 30 minutes after conditions deteriorated, raising questions about the timing of the closure.",
        "Thomas escaped without physical injuries, although González López said the experience left him visibly shaken. The hikers were lucky: the bridge's violent rotation happened after they had moved away, and had it happened a few minutes earlier, while Thomas was still mid-crossing, the outcome could have been very different.",
        "Torres del Paine, in southern Chile's Patagonia region, is one of South America's most popular hiking destinations, attracting visitors with its dramatic mountains, glaciers, lakes and extensive trekking routes. But the region is also known for rapidly changing weather conditions, and the park's wind is legendary among hikers. The latest scare comes just over a year after a deadly storm claimed the lives of five international tourists in the same national park. On November 17, 2025, a powerful weather system brought heavy snow, freezing temperatures and destructive winds to the O Circuit, trapping hikers in dangerous conditions near the Los Perros sector.",
        "The video, which circulated widely on November 25, has become a warning about how suddenly conditions can deteriorate in the remote Patagonian wilderness. For the park authorities, the questions now are about the bridge itself and about how quickly crossings are closed when the wind rises. For hikers planning the O Circuit, the lesson is simpler: in Torres del Paine, a calm crossing can turn dangerous in minutes, and the weather forecast is only the beginning of the story."
      ],
