      "slug": "ministers-ask-public-for-views-on-changing-age-of-consent",
      "sections": [
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "world",
      "headline": "Ministers ask public for views on changing age of consent",
      "dek": "Some campaigners have argued that different limits should apply, depending on the age gap between the people involved.",
      "date": "November 24, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwgkl31kmnddo",
      "body": [
        "The UK government has invited the public to share its views on whether the age of consent should be staggered, so that different limits apply depending on the age gap between the people involved. The move, announced on November 24, opens a public consultation on one of the most sensitive areas of criminal law, and ministers say they want survivors, parents, young people and victim support organisations to help shape it.",
        "At present, the legal age of consent is 16 across England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland. That means that, subject to existing legal protections, adults of any age can legally have sex with 16- and 17-year-olds, regardless of the age difference between them. A staggered system would change that, introducing additional limits based on how much older one person is than the other.",
        "The campaign group Close the Consent Gap has proposed a model under which a 16-year-old could not have a sexual relationship with anyone older than 19, and a 17-year-old could not have one with anyone older than 20. In other words, a gap of more than three years would make the relationship an offence, while teenagers would still be able to have relationships with people close to their own age. The government has not committed to adopting these specific limits, and is instead asking the public whether the law should change at all, and if so, how.",
        "The debate has gathered pace after a series of high-profile cases. Disgraced comedian Russell Brand was sentenced on November 9 to ten and a half years in prison after being convicted of one count of rape and three counts of sexual assault involving four women, with offences taking place between 2004 and 2009. Separately, Mia Latter, now 37, has alleged that Brand raped her when she was 16 and he was 30. Her allegation was not part of the criminal trial that led to his conviction, but she has since campaigned for changes to the age of consent, arguing that the law should give teenagers stronger protection against significantly older adults.",
        "Allegations have also been made against children's author and television personality David Walliams, 55, who has strongly denied them, including claims of grooming and predatory behavior. One woman alleged that Walliams began contacting her when she was 15 and he was 36. Ministers have pointed to these cases as evidence of how older men in positions of power can exploit teenagers, and Work and Pensions Secretary Pat McFadden described the reports as an appalling abuse of power.",
        "Prime Minister Andy Burnham said he wanted survivors and members of the public to share their experiences and help inform the process. First Secretary of State Louise Haigh told ITV News that the government did not want knee-jerk legislation on staggering the age of consent, but that the conversation around consent needed to happen. She said the government wanted to talk with young people about how they felt about the law as it stands, and about society's view of consent and healthy relationships.",
        "Equalities Minister Bridget Phillipson, speaking on BBC Breakfast, said the government needed to examine what was appropriate in relationships involving 16- and 17-year-olds, pointing to cases involving significant age differences and older men in positions of authority. She said the issue needed careful consideration rather than a rushed answer.",
        "Alongside the consultation, the government announced that new limits on non-disclosure agreements will come into force in January 2027, months earlier than originally planned. The changes will invalidate confidentiality clauses in new agreements if they prevent employees from talking about harassment or discrimination they have experienced or witnessed, and will ensure victims of crime can speak about their experiences, including to the media, even if they have previously signed an agreement. The announcement follows concerns that such agreements have been used to silence victims and protect powerful individuals.",
        "Ministers have acknowledged that changing the law would be complicated. Concerns have been raised about how a staggered system would work in practice: for example, if the age threshold were set at 19, a person in a relationship with a 16-year-old could potentially face criminalisation once they turned 20. Haigh acknowledged that such scenarios would need to be addressed during the consultation, and said it could be an ethical issue as much as a legal one. She also noted that changing consent laws would create further difficulties for a criminal justice system already under strain.",
        "The politics of the issue are also contested. Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch said there was some common sense about staggering the age of consent, but questioned whether a new law could be enforced. She also said it was very confusing to see Labor ministers talk about staggering the age of consent for sexual relations while at the same time lowering the voting age to 16. For now, the government has said it is actively looking at whether to change the law and will ask the public for its opinions in the new year, with no confirmed timeline for legislation. Minister for Victims Alex Davies-Jones said the measures form part of wider efforts to challenge myths about consent and halve violence against women and girls over the next decade."
      ],
