      "slug": "european-court-states-can-turn-back-migrants-in-some-cases",
      "sections": [
        "europe",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "europe",
      "headline": "European court: States can turn back migrants in some cases",
      "dek": "The European Court ruled that states belonging to the Council of Europe can turn back migrants, even under \"hostile\" conditions unless they've been identified as people who may be subject to ill-treatment.",
      "date": "November 24, 2026",
      "source": "DW",
      "source_url": "https://www.dw.com/en/european-court-states-can-turn-back-migrants-in-some-cases/a-80038352",
      "body": [
        "Europe’s top human rights court has ruled that states may turn back migrants at their borders in some circumstances, even without examining their asylum claims, in a landmark decision that redraws the legal line between border control and human rights.",
        "The Grand Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights, based in Strasbourg, delivered three judgments on Tuesday, November 24, in cases brought by migrants who were turned back at the borders of Poland, Latvia and Lithuania during the 2021 migration crisis, when thousands of people arrived from Belarus at the borders of the three European Union member states.",
        "The court found that Belarus had orchestrated the influx with the aim of destabilising its neighbours, a practice it described as the “instrumentalisation of migration.” In that context, it held, returning a foreign national to the state that is instrumentalising migration does not in itself violate Article 3 of the European Convention on Human Rights, which prohibits torture and inhuman or degrading treatment.",
        "That is a significant shift. Until now, the court’s case law had generally required states to examine asylum claims and to assess whether a person would face ill-treatment if returned. The new judgments say that, where migration is being used as a weapon by a third country, states do not have to assess whether the person would have access to an adequate asylum procedure in that state, because, the court reasoned, such an assessment “lacked pertinence” given the purpose of the policy and the manner of its implementation.",
        "But the court also drew a firm line. States must still identify two categories of people before turning anyone back: those with “evident personal vulnerabilities”, because of their age, their health or a disability, and those able to demonstrate a “real and immediate” risk of ill-treatment if returned. People in either group must be protected. The court also stressed that states retain their other obligations under international and European Union law, “in particular with respect to asylum,” a right the Convention itself does not protect.",
        "The three cases show how that test works in practice. In the case against Poland, the court found a violation of Article 3 for one Afghan applicant, who had crossed the border on October 20, 2021 and was removed to Belarus the same day despite an interim measure issued by the court barring his return. The court also found a violation of Article 34, which protects the right of individual petition. The case, A.R. and Others v. Poland, concerned Afghan citizens stranded near Usnarz Górny at the Polish-Belarusian border.",
        "In the case against Latvia, the court found a violation for five children and the relatives accompanying them, holding that their adequate protection could not be ensured without the presence and support of their close family members. It also ruled against Latvia over the undignified conditions imposed on families, including young children, stranded at the border. In the case against Lithuania, by contrast, the court found no violation for four Cuban applicants who had been turned back six times, because there was no evidence establishing their vulnerability or a concrete risk, although it did rule against Lithuania for detaining them for six months without a written decision or an effective remedy.",
        "The court ordered Poland, Latvia and Lithuania to pay compensation over their treatment of migrants at the EU’s external borders with Belarus. The judgments were reached by 11 votes to six, a division that underlines how contested the questions were even within the court itself.",
        "Reaction was swift and divided. The Helsinki Foundation for Human Rights, which helped bring the Polish case, said the ruling was the first to define states’ obligations when another state instrumentalises migration, but warned that it limited protection against onward expulsion. Governments in the region are likely to welcome the decision as legal backing for the hardline border policies they adopted in 2021 and have maintained since, including the right, as the court put it, to restrict entry or even fully close their borders.",
        "For migrants and their advocates, the concern is the opposite: that the ruling gives states a legal framework for pushbacks, provided they can show they checked for vulnerability first. The court’s answer is that the check is not optional, it is the condition on which the whole ruling rests. Whether that condition is honoured in practice, at remote border crossings in the middle of the night, will be the real test of what the Strasbourg judges decided.",
        "Sources: Dawn, Agence Europe and the European Court of Human Rights press releases."
      ],
      "related": [],
