      "slug": "triple-killer-admits-murdering-ian-huntley-in-jail",
      "sections": [
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "world",
      "headline": "Triple killer admits murdering Ian Huntley in jail",
      "dek": "Anthony Russell changes his plea on the second day of his trial at Newcastle Crown Court.",
      "date": "November 24, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm930y07p0j2o",
      "body": [
        "A prisoner already serving a life sentence for three murders has admitted murdering the Soham killer Ian Huntley in a high-security jail, and has been given a second whole-life order, meaning he will never be released. Anthony Russell, 44, changed his plea on the second day of his trial at Newcastle Crown Court, admitting a deliberate and premeditated killing.",
        "Russell struck Huntley, 52, in the recycling workshop at HMP Frankland, a Category A high-security prison near Durham, on the morning of February 26. He hit Huntley on the head at least six times with a 4kg metal bar while the two men were on work duty together. Huntley never regained consciousness and died in hospital nine days later, on March 7, having sustained a fractured skull and a brain bleed.",
        "The trial began at Newcastle Crown Court on Monday, with prosecutors describing the killing as a cold-blooded murder that had been planned for weeks. On Tuesday, Russell asked to change his plea and admitted the single charge of murder. Sentencing him later the same day, Mr Justice Hilliard told him: this was premeditated murder by a man who had murdered three times before. The judge concluded that Russell would never be released and would never be eligible to apply to the parole board.",
        "The court heard that Russell had planned the murder for weeks and had told a governor he was sick of Huntley. Matthew Bean KC, for the prosecution, told the jury that Russell had told the staff member: he put it on me about six months ago and I walked away, but when he sees weakness in people he tries to bully them. Russell later told police: he's been getting really arrogant, cocky, in my face, winding me up, just little smiles as if to say yeah sort of thing, and I'd just had enough of it. Christopher Atkinson, from the Crown Prosecution Service, said Russell had selected a weapon and concealed it before waiting for the opportunity to strike, and that Russell claimed he had nothing to lose by carrying out the attack.",
        "There had been tension between the two men for about a year before the murder. They were held together on A wing at the high-security prison, and in March 2025 they had to be separated after an argument in which they threatened one another. Footage of the aftermath of the fatal attack showed Russell shouting Ian Huntley's dead and saying he was proud of it as he was marched through a prison yard.",
        "Russell had first denied murder and offered a plea to manslaughter on the grounds of diminished responsibility. He said he had been hearing voices and suffered from post-traumatic stress disorder related to childhood trauma. He abandoned that position after the prosecution outlined evidence of preparation spanning several weeks. His barrister, Tyrone Smith, said Russell accepted that the killing of Huntley was deliberate and planned, and that he felt no remorse towards him, but that he had apologised to the people who witnessed the attack and had to deal with its consequences.",
        "Huntley was serving a life sentence with a minimum term of 40 years for murdering Holly Wells and Jessica Chapman, both ten, in Soham, Cambridgeshire, in 2002. His death closes the case of one of Britain's most notorious child killers, but the court heard nothing that lessened the gravity of what Russell had done. The judge emphasised that the enmity between the two convicted murderers in no way diminishes the gravity of the crime.",
        "Russell was already serving a whole-life prison tariff, imposed in 2021, for three murders committed in October 2020. He was sentenced in March 2022 for the murders of Julie Williams, 58, and her son David, 32, at separate flats in Coventry, as well as the rape and murder of Nicole McGregor, 31, who was pregnant, in woodland near Leamington Spa. A whole-life order means the prisoner will never be released, and the jury was told that the phrase natural lifer referred to Russell's whole-life sentence.",
        "The second whole-life order is exceptionally rare. It means that even if the first sentence were ever reviewed, Russell would still have no prospect of release. The Crown Prosecution Service said the attack was a deliberate and planned one in which Russell selected a weapon and concealed it, before waiting for his opportunity. Russell, who had already killed three times, will now spend the rest of his life in prison with no possibility of parole."
      ],
