      "headline": "China sentences official to death for spying for foreign country",
      "dek": "Accused official leaked national secrets to unnamed country and received payments worth over $150,000, state-linked publisher says.",
      "date": "November 24, 2026",
      "source": "The Guardian",
      "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/24/china-official-ma-death-sentence-spying-leaking-national-secrets",
      "body": [
        "A Chinese government official has been sentenced to death for spying for a foreign country, according to a statement published by Gold Wall Press, a publishing company associated with China’s National Administration of State Secrets Protection. The official, identified only by the surname Ma, was accused of leaking national secrets in exchange for payments worth more than one million yuan, about $150,000.",
        "According to the account, Ma was approached and recruited by a foreign intelligence service while working at an overseas government posting. The recruiter, an operative identified as Kailin, first made contact by saying she wanted to learn Chinese, according to CNN’s reporting of the statement, and gradually steered their conversations towards politically sensitive topics and major international developments.",
        "Kailin then began asking for sensitive information about the personnel and operations of the Chinese institution where Ma worked, including its internal telephone directory. When Ma hesitated at riskier requests, Kailin introduced another operative, identified as Tang Zheng, who threatened to report Ma’s earlier disclosures to the Chinese authorities unless he agreed to work for them.",
        "Ma then signed a formal cooperation agreement containing clauses on “confidentiality” and “loyalty”, and was promised fixed monthly payments. He was also given a dedicated laptop for communicating with his handlers. After completing his overseas assignment and returning to a central government role in China, he continued passing information, copying material from classified documents he encountered in his regular duties during the day and sending it abroad at night through a website run by the spy service.",
        "Investigators found that Ma provided more than 100 items of information to his handlers. Of those, 35 were classified as state secrets, including nine at the highest level and 26 classed as confidential, according to accounts of the case. He also met his handlers inside China and abroad to collect regular payments and bonuses, and financial records showed he received more than one million yuan in total.",
        "The court convicted Ma of espionage and sentenced him to death, with lifelong deprivation of political rights and confiscation of all his personal property. The statement did not say whether the sentence has already been carried out, and it did not identify the court, the government department where Ma worked, or when the trial and sentencing took place.",
        "Crucially, the account comes entirely from Chinese state-linked sources and could not be checked independently. The statement did not name the foreign country involved, and Reuters reported that it could not independently verify the account. That leaves the most basic questions about the case, who recruited Ma, for whom, and exactly what he handed over, resting on a single official narrative.",
        "The case was made public in the same week that China’s anti-corruption agency announced an investigation into Chen Jining, the Communist Party secretary of Shanghai and a Politburo member, on suspicion of violating party discipline, a reminder that Beijing’s internal security apparatus is active on several fronts at once. Gold Wall Press described the Ma case as a “wake-up call” about the efforts of what it called “cunning” foreign adversaries to recruit Chinese assets.",
        "China has in recent years stepped up its warnings about foreign espionage, with state security agencies regularly publishing accounts of alleged recruitment attempts, often in language designed as much for domestic deterrence as for public information. The Ma case follows that pattern: a detailed, dramatic narrative, published by a state-linked outlet, with the most sensitive details, the country, the ministry, the court, withheld.",
        "For now, the case stands as a public warning rather than a fully verifiable account. Until Chinese authorities release more detail, or independent evidence emerges, what is known is the shape of the story as Beijing has chosen to tell it: an official recruited abroad, paid to betray secrets, caught, and sentenced to the harshest punishment Chinese law allows."
      ],
