      "section": "technology",
      "headline": "US to blacklist Asian AI firm Megaspeed after Nvidia scandal",
      "dek": "Megaspeed has been accused of funneling AI chips to China after a former executive's affair with Jensen Huang became public.",
      "date": "November 23, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cv98xpg5dg5zo",
      "body": [
        "The United States has blacklisted the Singapore-based AI firm Megaspeed after a scandal involving Nvidia, the world's most valuable chipmaker, and the alleged smuggling of advanced American chips to China. The move, by the Commerce Department, bars US companies from selling Megaspeed chips and other technology, and it comes at the center of a political and corporate storm that has already cost Nvidia's chief executive his job once.",
        "The Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security said it was blacklisting Megaspeed due to suspicions it was acting contrary to the national security or foreign policy interests of the United States, citing a significant risk of involvement in chip diversion. The decision followed months of reported disagreement inside the administration over restricting the firm.",
        "The evidence behind the decision was laid out by Bloomberg News, which reported that banned Nvidia Blackwell chips had been found at a Shanghai data center linked to Megaspeed and its former executive Alice Huang. A pair of Chinese firms installed the powerful Blackwell semiconductors at the Yangtze River Delta Project, a data center linked to Huang. The gear installed there included a GB300 NVL72 rack, among Nvidia's most powerful products, according to a serial number in documents submitted to Chinese regulators in November. Shanghai Shuoyao offered the hardware as collateral to obtain a 96 million yuan, or 14 million dollar, loan from Shanghai Innovation Bank, according to filings reviewed by Bloomberg News.",
        "A second firm, Shanghai Kezhan, described a GB200 server, which also relies on Blackwell chips, at the facility, in documents filed when it obtained a 90 million yuan loan in late 2025. The same firm also disclosed that it has 32 servers with H200 chips there. Shanghai Longjie, which has the same parent company as Shanghai Shuoyao, disclosed that it has servers containing Nvidia's earlier Hopper-generation chips, which are illegal to ship to China without Washington's permission.",
        "Bloomberg traced the GB300 rack through its serial number, entering it into a public warranty database and finding that it was manufactured by Taiwan's Gigabyte Technology, which assembles Nvidia chips into servers and racks. The links between the firms are extensive: Shanghai Shuoyao, which procured the GB300 rack, is active at the data center linked to Alice Huang and has significant overlap with Megaspeed, and confirmed this month that it is one of Megaspeed's service providers. Megaspeed used a rendering of the Yangtze River Delta Project in 2024 to describe its largest data center to investors, without disclosing the location in China. Bloomberg does not have evidence that Megaspeed itself diverted Nvidia hardware there, and Megaspeed has denied wrongdoing.",
        "Alice Huang founded Megaspeed in 2023, and the firm, a so-called neocloud, won a coveted stamp of approval from Nvidia within four days of its creation and went on to become the chipmaker's largest Southeast Asian customer. She is a former Megaspeed executive who is suspected of having worked with Chinese intelligence during an affair with Nvidia chief executive Jensen Huang. The two share a surname but are not related. Commerce officials first opened an investigation into Megaspeed in 2024, resulting in an attempted interview of Alice Huang in June 2025, the same month that Nvidia put her company on its no sell list.",
        "The affair has shaken Nvidia itself. Jensen Huang was ousted following revelations of two affairs, including one with a suspected Chinese intelligence asset, and the board unanimously accepted his resignation and told investors he would not return. But on Monday morning, before the market opened, Nvidia announced that Huang was back as chief executive, after a frantic White House push led by President Donald Trump and Vice President JD Vance. The nation's top law enforcement officer informed Trump and Vance that Huang was not the target or subject of any Justice Department investigation, and that the department had no evidence to suggest he had broken laws or was a national security risk. Huang was contrite, and the board decided having him return was the best path. Nvidia shares quickly recovered much of their roughly 30 per cent decline since Huang's resignation.",
        "The investigations are far from over. The Justice Department is leading two probes of Nvidia customers about suspected chip smuggling, into Megaspeed and Super Micro, a maker of data center servers linked to chip smuggling allegations in the past, and Nvidia has cooperated for months with the investigations. Lawmakers are scheduled to hear from Huang next week, and have also called for their own investigations.",
        "The scandal has exposed how porous the controls on advanced chips can be. President Donald Trump has explicitly said he does not want Blackwell chips, which OpenAI and Anthropic use to train their most cutting-edge models, going to China, at least for now, and has suggested they would not become available for another two or three years. Nvidia's chief executive once claimed the racks were too big to smuggle: a standard two-metre-tall GB300 NVL72 rack, containing 72 Blackwell Ultra chips and 36 Grace processors, weighs more than 1,500 kilograms and is about the size of a gigantic fridge. Yet the filings reviewed by Bloomberg show that restricted hardware has reached China, and that some Chinese financial institutions, including ones with government ties, were aware of it and willing to lend capital on those assets. Questions remain about why it took months to blacklist Megaspeed."
      ],
