      "section": "war-conflict",
      "headline": "US escalates sanctions on Iran, targets ‘shadow banking’ networks",
      "dek": "US Treasury sanctions over two dozen Iranian entities, citing efforts to disrupt Tehran's global financial activities.",
      "date": "November 23, 2026",
      "source": "Al Jazeera",
      "source_url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/23/us-escalates-sanctions-on-iran-targets-shadow-banking-networks",
      "body": [
        "The United States has launched another wave of sanctions against Iran, targeting more than two dozen exchange houses, front companies and individuals that Washington says form the country’s “shadow banking” network. The Treasury Department announced the measures on Monday, November 23, describing them as a sweeping effort to disrupt Iran’s global financial activities, amid an ongoing diplomatic effort to end the US-Israel war on Iran.",
        "The action, carried out by the Treasury’s Office of Foreign Assets Control, is part of Operation Economic Outcast, the whole-of-government campaign to sever the economic lifelines that sustain the Iranian regime. It was taken under three executive orders, 13902, which targets sectors of the Iranian economy including the financial sector; 13224, a counterterrorism designation authority; and 13871, which targets persons operating in sectors of Iran’s metals industry.",
        "“Iran’s shadow banking system is the last financial lifeline of a bankrupt regime,” said US Secretary of the Treasury Scott Bessent. “Treasury will continue dismantling these illicit networks until we have buried the head of the snake.” Bessent also said that “Iran’s economy is underwater, and its leaders are panicking” over the worsening economy, as Washington tightened its financial squeeze.",
        "The Treasury said the designated entities help Iran “move billions of dollars through the international financial system to provide the regime access to foreign currency despite comprehensive sanctions”. The networks are overseen by the Central Bank of Iran, according to the Treasury statement, and operate through Iran-based foreign exchange houses and affiliated front companies in several jurisdictions, most prominently Dubai and Hong Kong, as well as Türkiye.",
        "Among the targets was the Iran Center for Exchange of Currency and Gold, which the Treasury says matches Iranian exporters earning foreign currency with importers who need it, and serves tens of billions of dollars in trade annually. Another was an Iran-based company named Rahbar Bazargani Nesim Baharan Qeshm Company, which the Treasury claims worked closely with Iranian exchange houses and their front companies to facilitate transactions on behalf of clients of Sarmayeh Bank, including major Iranian petrochemical companies. Mina Fashki and Partners Exchange Company and Mellat Exchange Company, along with a number of their owners and employees, were also designated.",
        "The Treasury also designated Dubai-based H N B Trading, which it said helped arrange a payment for a ship charter involving a company linked to Iran’s Armed Forces General Staff. In total, the statement listed 16 companies in the United Arab Emirates, including firms in Dubai and the Dubai Multi Commodities Center that Treasury said had handled payments linked to Iranian oil, petrochemical and metals entities, including the National Iranian Oil Company and the National Iranian Tanker Company, as well as Grandez Trading Limited, described as a Hong Kong-based front company, and a Turkish customs and trading company. The Gulf News reported that the Office of Foreign Assets Control added 22 entities and eight people to its sanctions list.",
        "Alongside the designations, the Treasury’s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network released an analysis identifying nearly $15 billion of financial activity conducted from January 2025 to August 2026 that it said was potentially linked to Iranian shadow banking. The analysis flagged several major transaction routes, including around $2.6 billion in transfers from banks in mainland China to banks in the United Arab Emirates, approximately $981 million from Singapore to the UAE, and around $922 million from the United Kingdom to the UAE.",
        "The sanctions come as the United States expands its pressure on Iran on several fronts at once. On November 7, US Central Command announced the formation of Task Force Iron Gate, an internal staff of around 20 civilian and military personnel based in Tampa, Florida, to target land routes and the movement of military equipment and restricted materials through Iran’s land routes and the Caspian Sea. The new measures follow that military step with a financial one, and they come shortly after President Donald Trump threatened renewed military action next year.",
        "Iran’s response has been defiant. Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said a land blockade would be “almost impossible”, as the US targets Iranian supply routes, maritime trade and alleged shadow banking networks. The sanctions are designed to make it harder for Iran to earn and move foreign currency, and to raise the cost of the trade that keeps its economy running, but they also raise the stakes in a confrontation that has already produced a war, a blockade and a financial siege.",
        "What happens next will depend on whether the sanctions bite. The networks Washington is targeting are, by design, hard to see and hard to dismantle: front companies, exchange houses and intermediaries spread across several jurisdictions, moving money through channels that are difficult to trace. The Treasury’s message is that it will keep going until the networks are gone. Iran’s message is that it will find another way. The contest between those two claims will play out in the months ahead, in bank accounts as much as on battlefields."
      ],
