      "slug": "i-want-to-destroy-capitalism-not-france-on-the-streets-with",
      "sections": [
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "world",
      "headline": "'I want to destroy capitalism, not France': On the streets with the French ultra left",
      "dek": "The BBC meets the far-left protesters French President Emmanuel Macron says are trying to bring down the Republic.",
      "date": "November 23, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cw074km48myeo",
      "body": [
        "“I want to destroy capitalism, not France.” It is the line that gives the BBC's Katya Adler her title, and it is the sentence that sums up the young protesters she met on the streets of France, angry, masked, and convinced that the system, not the country, is their enemy.",
        "France is in turmoil. Crippling public debt, a president with six months left in office, and massive social unrest, sparked by student protests which have erupted into looting and violence. This Saturday marked the sixth weekend in a row of demonstrations against soaring prices, worsening public services and alleged police brutality. Violence by protesters is down on last month but still present.",
        "President Emmanuel Macron has publicly blamed the riots on “delinquents” and “ultra-left groups” whose aim, he says, is to bring down the French Republic. That is quite a claim, and one that needs examining, because the unrest has become a political football ahead of key presidential and parliamentary elections next year. The left blames the center and the right for the cost-of-living crisis making French people angry; the center and right blame the hard left for fomenting violence, which the left denies; and the nationalist right pledges, if elected, to return order and security to France's streets.",
        "So who exactly are these “ultra-left” groups that Macron talks about, and how organized and dangerous are they? It is infamously hard to speak to members of the radical left. They do not much like what they call mainstream media, which they view as part of an establishment many of them despise. Adler's best bet was to head out into the protests and try to approach people dressed in all black, with ski masks or balaclavas worn, they say, to hide their identity from police. It is a quasi-uniform associated with left-wing rioters or disruptors, known as the Black Bloc, seen at demonstrations all over Europe. In France, they tend to appear from city side streets as daytime protests die down, dusk falls and riot police emerge in full force.",
        "French intelligence drastically underestimated the outpouring of anger, looting and destruction that followed last month's protests. More than 1,000 police officers were reportedly injured, young protesters had bones broken, and two reportedly lost a hand in altercations with police armed with stun grenades. Le Monde newspaper maintains an article it constantly updates to investigate a rising number of reports of protester injuries. The French Insurers Federation estimates that damage to shops and private property on October 17 alone, the day of the biggest riots, came to around €207 million.",
        "Towards the end of one of the protests this month, Adler saw would-be rioters vastly outnumbered, glass bottles and homemade paint bombs at the ready to throw at police. As she was being forcefully kettled in by police, she tried to talk to them. Few wanted to engage, but then she met 18-year-old Nas, not his real name, and his friends.",
        "“How can you justify violence?” she asked them. “Without violence, nothing can be achieved,” one of them told her. “It's the only way that they can listen to us.” Nas said they came from an area with a bad reputation. “Our schools are a disaster. The teachers resign. Qualifications are needed to get a decent job. There is equality in France. We will not abandon our struggle,” he added. These young people, Adler discovered, come from the tower blocks typical of the outskirts of French cities, the banlieues associated with poverty, instability and second- or third-generation immigrant families. They insisted they did not answer to a political party or a radical leftist organisation, but were simply angry. In France today, they say, true violence is state violence.",
        "The authorities tell a different story about the same streets. Reuters, which reviewed dozens of videos of the violence, attended the court hearings of 26 people accused of taking part and interviewed more than 50 officials, police officers, attorneys, protesters and witnesses, found that the movement began in September with small protests over conditions in state schools and spiralled into some of the worst civil unrest in years. Images of riot police baton-charging teenagers fanned support from parents, teachers, left-wing politicians and trade unionists. On October 17, police lost control of entire neighbourhoods in Paris, Marseille and Lyon. On October 24, far-right gangs entered the fray, saying they wanted to protect businesses, and clashed with protesters in several cities. The Interior Ministry says its internal watchdogs have launched 161 investigations into allegations of officer violence against protesters since September.",
        "French intelligence estimates there are 5,000 “ultra-left” activists in the country. Whether that number justifies Macron's claim that they aim to bring down the Republic is the question Adler's reporting leaves open. What is clear is that the unrest has not stopped: every week since October 17, people have taken to the streets across France to voice their anger at police and the government, and the demonstrations show no sign of abating before next year's presidential election.",
        "Sources: BBC reporting by Katya Adler, and Reuters."
      ],
      "related": [],
