      "slug": "who-chief-calls-for-peace-amid-heavy-fighting-in-ebola-hit",
      "sections": [
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "world",
      "headline": "WHO chief calls for peace amid heavy fighting in Ebola-hit DR Congo region",
      "dek": "Fighting near a DRC hospital disrupts Ebola efforts, putting patients and workers at 'grave risk', WHO warns.",
      "date": "November 23, 2026",
      "source": "Al Jazeera",
      "source_url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/23/who-chief-calls-for-peace-amid-heavy-fighting-in-ebola-hit-dr-congo-region",
      "body": [
        "The head of the World Health Organization has called for peace after heavy fighting in the Democratic Republic of the Congo’s Ituri province disrupted the response to the country’s Ebola outbreak and forced health workers to shelter inside a maternity ward for more than 24 hours. Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said the clashes, which began on Saturday near a hospital in the area where the outbreak first emerged, had put patients, families and health workers at grave risk.",
        "“Heavy fighting has been ongoing since yesterday in the vicinity of a hospital in Ituri, in an area close to where the Ebola outbreak first emerged,” Tedros said in a post on X on Sunday. “The situation has disrupted the work of the hospital and the wider Ebola response, putting patients, families and health workers at grave risk.” He added: “The best medicine is peace.”",
        "Medical staff and aid partners sheltered inside the maternity ward for more than 24 hours, and the WHO said it was working to arrange a safe exit for the team and for the hospital’s facilities, staff and patients to be protected. Neither the hospital nor the armed groups involved were named in the initial reports. On Tuesday, Tedros confirmed that WHO personnel, Congolese health ministry staff and their colleagues had been safely evacuated from the hospital in Akwe, where they had sought shelter for more than three days.",
        "The fighting is part of a wider escalation. Tedros said the frequency of attacks across the country’s east had increased since the end of October, seriously affecting the Ebola response. The renewed fighting between government forces and the armed group known as the CRP has spilled into areas at the heart of the outbreak, disrupting operations and forcing international health workers to evacuate. The WHO has also temporarily relocated its Ebola response team and partners from Mongbwalu, one of the original epicentres of the outbreak, due to heightened insecurity.",
        "The intensified fighting, with the armed group advancing towards Bunia, the provincial capital of Ituri, raised fears of a wider escalation. In a statement issued on Tuesday, the CRP announced that it would halt its advance towards Bunia and withdraw from recently seized areas, citing commitments made during Uganda-facilitated talks with the Congolese authorities. But the Congolese military accused the armed group on the same day of continuing attacks against civilians and military positions despite its declared unilateral ceasefire, alleging targeted killings, abductions and ambushes.",
        "The outbreak itself is the largest and deadliest Ebola outbreak the country has ever recorded. As of November 17, the WHO reported 5,678 deaths out of 11,613 cases, and later figures put the toll at more than 5,800 deaths among nearly 12,000 cases. The outbreak, caused by the Bundibugyo species of the virus, was declared on May 15, and has now been running for more than six months. Ituri and North Kivu remain the most affected provinces.",
        "Last week, the WHO maintained the outbreak’s status as a public health emergency of international concern, identifying worsening insecurity as the biggest impediment to expanding response operations. In an update issued on Saturday, the WHO said some areas had seen a slight fall in cases, but that high-level transmission was continuing across the country as a whole. The outbreak remains out of control, with case numbers and deaths rising at a record pace, according to Reuters.",
        "The collision of war and disease is the cruelest part of the story. Ebola response depends on contact tracing, safe burials, vaccination and the rapid isolation of cases, all of which require health workers to move freely, communities to trust them, and hospitals to function. When fighting forces those workers to shelter in a maternity ward for three days, the virus does not wait. Every day of disruption is a day in which chains of transmission go untraced and cases go uncounted.",
        "Tedros has determined that the outbreak remains a public health emergency of international concern, and the WHO is working with humanitarian partners to secure a safe exit for staff trapped at the facility and to protect hospital workers, patients and healthcare facilities. But the agency cannot protect a hospital from a war, and it cannot run an Ebola response in a province where the fighting is getting worse.",
        "The best medicine, as Tedros said, is peace. Until it comes, the health workers of Ituri will keep doing what they have done for six months: treating the sick, burying the dead, and hoping that the next day is quieter than the last."
      ],
