      "slug": "russia-bound-ship-towed-away-after-it-was-attacked-in-gulf",
      "sections": [
        "war-conflict",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "war-conflict",
      "headline": "Russia-bound ship towed away after it was attacked in Gulf of Finland",
      "dek": "Finnish defense forces say that Ukraine was likely behind the drone strike on Saturday. Kyiv has yet to comment.",
      "date": "November 22, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cj752ddve27ko",
      "body": [
        "A Russian cargo ship bound for the exclave of Kaliningrad has been towed out of Finnish territorial waters after being struck by drones in the Gulf of Finland, in an attack Finland says was likely carried out by Ukraine. The vessel, the Sparta II, caught fire, its crew was evacuated, and the incident has brought the war in Ukraine to Finland's doorstep.",
        "The Sparta II is a 121-metre roll-on/roll-off cargo vessel, sailing under a Russian flag and owned by Oboronlogistika, a Russian shipping company that provides logistics services for the Russian Defense Ministry. It left St Petersburg on Friday, bound for Kaliningrad, the Russian exclave on the Baltic coast. On Saturday morning, Finland's Ministry of Defense said its forces had detected a drone attack on the vessel in international waters west of Gogland Island, off the Finnish town of Loviisa. A photograph taken by a Finnish patrol plane showed flames and thick smoke coming from the rear of the ship.",
        "The governor of Russia's Leningrad region, Alexander Drozdenko, said the vessel had been attacked by two drones about three nautical miles, or 5.6 kilometres, from Russia's border. Finnish authorities said the ship was damaged in international waters and later drifted into Finnish territory, where it anchored off Loviisa. Firefighting efforts were still going on at sea, with rescue units and patrol ships from the Finnish Border Guard, a patrol aircraft and helicopters, two Finnish civilian rescue tugs and an Estonian navy patrol ship at the scene. At the request of the Border Guard, the Russian rescue ship Murman also took part in the rescue operation.",
        "The 16 crew members were evacuated onto a nearby cargo ship and were to be taken directly back to Russia. A fire aboard the Sparta II continued to burn through the night despite efforts to extinguish it, the Finnish coastguard said, and thermal cameras showed that some containers were still hot. By Sunday, the vessel was stable enough for towing to begin, and a Russian vessel towed it back into Russian waters, where it is now anchored in St Petersburg.",
        "By Saturday afternoon, the Finnish Defense Forces said the strike was likely carried out by Ukraine. Kyiv did not comment at first, but on Monday President Volodymyr Zelensky confirmed in an interview in Kyiv that Ukraine was responsible. He said the vessel was a legitimate military target and that it was carrying weapons, although the news agency reporting his remarks said it could not independently verify that claim. He acknowledged that the attack might have rattled some of Ukraine's allies, but said: we did everything right. Norway, a strong supporter of Kyiv, urged it to exercise caution in such operations.",
        "The incident also produced a second flashpoint. A Russian helicopter violated Finnish airspace, venturing more than four kilometres into Finnish territory, in what the Finnish Defense Forces said was possibly related to rescue or tracking operations for the damaged cargo ship. Finland quickly implemented temporary air and sea traffic restrictions in the eastern part of the gulf, to protect civilians and allow the authorities to operate if drones needed to be intercepted. Finland's Prime Minister Petteri Orpo wrote on social media that war was spilling over into Finland's immediate vicinity, adding that Ukraine had the right to fight back against the aggressor.",
        "The reason the ship was targeted lies in its links to the Russian military. Finland's Border Guard said the Sparta II is known to have links to Russia's armed forces, and the vessel is subject to US sanctions. According to the OpenSanctions database, it has been involved in transporting weapons, notably from Syria to Russia via the Bosphorus and Dardanelles straits. Commander Ilja Iljin of the Finnish Border Guard said there was no confirmed information about what cargo was on board, but that it could not be ruled out that there was military materiel on board. Kaliningrad's Governor Alexey Besprozvannykh said supplies to the exclave were not at risk.",
        "The attack is part of a widening pattern. Earlier in November, Finland's military said it had detected a strike on a Russian warship in the same area, which Ukraine took credit for, and Helsinki later said it had raised concerns with Kyiv about strikes there. Ukraine and Russia have attacked each other's shipping throughout the war, mostly in the Black Sea, but the Baltic is one of the most crowded shipping lanes in the world: the Helsinki to Tallinn ferry route alone had 7.5 million passenger crossings last year. There is also an environmental risk, since one of the major dangers of attacking any vessel is damage to the environment if it leaks or sinks, a scenario Baltic Sea nations treat with high importance, and the risk is further amplified by the poor state of some of the so-called shadow fleet tankers Russia operates. Estonian Navy Commander Ivo Vark said the risk of further attacks in the Gulf of Finland could not be ruled out. Accounts differ on the precise location of the strike: Finnish authorities place it in international waters west of Gogland Island, while Zelensky said the vessel was targeted in Russian territorial waters before drifting into Finnish waters."
      ],
