      "slug": "briton-swims-length-of-strait-of-hormuz-to-highlight",
      "sections": [
        "war-conflict",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "war-conflict",
      "headline": "Briton swims length of strait of Hormuz to highlight environmental cost of war",
      "dek": "UN patron Lewis Pugh saw ships ablaze and fighter jets overhead during three-day swim as Iran conflict continues.",
      "date": "November 22, 2026",
      "source": "The Guardian",
      "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/22/briton-lewis-pugh-swims-strait-of-hormuz-environment-war",
      "body": [
        "British endurance swimmer Lewis Pugh has completed a three-day swim through the Strait of Hormuz, watching oil tankers burn and fighter jets pass overhead, in a protest aimed at drawing attention to the environmental cost of the war over Iran. The 56-year-old swam about 15 kilometres (9.3 miles) between November 15 and 18, from Musandam Island to the Cape of Hormuz at the northern tip of Oman’s Musandam Peninsula, accompanied by a small expedition team, a support vessel and a kayak.",
        "Thirty minutes into the first day, Pugh heard two explosions and looked up to see an oil tanker about four kilometres away that had been hit. “The explosion was so loud, two booms, and then fighter jets coming in. And at that moment I realised we have to get out of here right now,” he told the Associated Press. His team paused the swim until the immediate danger had passed.",
        "On the second day, he saw flames rising from a liquefied petroleum gas carrier, the Tara Gas, that had been struck. “The flames were enormous,” Pugh said. “These vessels carry highly flammable gases, and an attack on one can have catastrophic consequences, not just for crew members, but also on the surrounding marine environment.” He later passed close by the vessel while it was still on fire, and said his first thoughts were for the seafarers on board.",
        "Pugh, a United Nations Patron of the Oceans, undertook the swim to highlight what he calls the forgotten casualty of war: nature itself. “War causes immense suffering. People are killed and families are torn apart. Hospitals, schools and homes can be destroyed in minutes,” he told the UN Environment Program. “But there’s another casualty that receives far too little attention, and that is nature itself.”",
        "The Strait of Hormuz is one of the world’s most important waterways, carrying a large share of the oil shipped from the Middle East, and the surrounding Persian Gulf is especially vulnerable to pollution. It is a shallow, semi-enclosed basin with high temperatures, high evaporation and extreme salinity, and water leaves it only through the narrow strait. Hydrocarbons released there tend to persist for many years, spreading across borders on currents and tides.",
        "Its ecosystems were already under pressure before the war. Mangroves, wetlands, coral communities and habitats for dugongs, dolphins and humpback whales are affected by pollution from oil and gas production, storage and transport, as well as industry, desalination plants and oil spills. During 2026, according to the UN Environment Program, those pressures increased further as marine traffic accumulated and conflict added new damage.",
        "The scale of the attacks is striking. At least 175 vessels have been attacked since the fighting began, and about 30 of those attacks released significant pollution, according to figures cited in coverage of the swim. The Washington Post reported, citing data from the Washington Institute for Near East Policy, that vessels passing through the strait came under attack 16 times in the first 16 days of November alone.",
        "The swim took place inside a wider struggle over the strait itself. According to reporting on the expedition, the war broke out on February 28, when US-Israeli strikes killed Iran’s supreme leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, and a June deal sketching a path to a permanent settlement collapsed. Iran moved to block the Strait of Hormuz, and the United States answered with a naval blockade of Iranian ports, a raft of economic sanctions and a task force aimed at land routes into Iran.",
        "Pugh is no stranger to extreme swims. He was the first person to complete a long-distance swim in every ocean of the world and the first to swim across the North Pole, and he has previously swum in a glacial lake on Mount Everest and in Antarctica, using each swim to campaign for the protection of marine environments. This was, however, his first swim in a conflict zone, and he is likely the only person to have attempted a long-distance swim in the Strait of Hormuz since the war began.",
        "His message, he said, was simple. “We’ve got to stop the war. We’ve got to open the strait, and we’ve got to clean up the pollution,” he told the Washington Post. Writing on X after the swim, he added: “The environmental cost of war is immense. It outlasts every ceasefire. Oil in the sea, toxins in the soil, reefs destroyed. Wounds that take generations to heal. The children who inherit them did not start these wars, but they will live with the consequences.”",
        "Whether that message reaches the people who can act on it is another matter. The strait remains contested, the blockade remains in place, and every week, as Pugh put it, dozens of oil tankers, gas carriers and cargo ships are being targeted. For now, his swim has at least forced an environmental story that had gone largely untold, covered by Al Jazeera, the Associated Press, Sky News and the BBC, into the open, and put the damage to the sea on the same agenda as the damage to the people who live beside it."
      ],
