      "slug": "just-wrong-british-right-distances-itself-from-trump-after",
      "sections": [
        "us",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "us",
      "headline": "‘Just wrong’: British right distances itself from Trump after Putin ‘friend’ post",
      "dek": "Reform deputy leader calls Russian leader ‘a murderous dictator’ after US president appears to endorse him. UK politics live, latest updates.",
      "date": "November 22, 2026",
      "source": "The Guardian",
      "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/nov/22/british-right-reform-tory-party-distances-itself-from-trump-putin-friend-post",
      "body": [
        "Britain’s Reform UK and the Conservative Party have distanced themselves from Donald Trump after the US president posted a photograph of himself shaking hands with Vladimir Putin, captioned “My friend”. The image, shared on Trump’s Truth Social platform overnight, showed him leaning towards the Russian leader as they met at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit in Shenzhen, China, where Trump also met China’s leader, Xi Jinping.",
        "The reaction from Reform UK, the British party most closely associated with Trump, was blunt. Asked for his reaction, the party’s deputy leader, Richard Tice, replied: “Horror.” He told Sky News: “It’s just wrong at every level ... And it’s a grave mistake. He is not our friend, Putin. He is, as others have said, a murderous dictator. And no one is in any doubt about that.”",
        "Tice was then asked whether Nigel Farage, the Reform UK leader and a friend of Trump, would tell the US president that he had made an error. “There have been a number of occasions where one can agree with Trump and then disagree with him,” Tice said. “There have been more occasions recently when clearly we have disagreed with him and said so very explicitly, and in a sense that’s the nature of long friendships. Sometimes you’ll agree, and sometimes you’ll disagree, and I think the disagreements are growing.”",
        "The government’s response was equally sharp. Hamish Falconer, the European relations minister, called Putin a “butcher” and said he was “not a friend of the UK”. Speaking on Sky News’s Sunday Morning with Michael Gove, Falconer said: “One area where the UK is absolutely and completely united across the whole political spectrum is standing with Ukraine against Russia’s barbaric, illegal war. The cost has been so high... Russia is suffering terribly from a war which is illegal, it is brutal, it is starving Russian young people of so many opportunities.”",
        "Asked whether Putin would be his friend, Falconer answered: “No. And he’s not a friend of the UK either.” He insisted that the prime minister had made Britain’s position clear to Mr Trump, saying: “The Prime Minister speaks to the president regularly, and is in absolutely no way pulling his punches about the strength of feeling that the UK has as a whole about Russia’s invasion of Ukraine.”",
        "The episode matters because of who is doing the distancing. Reform and the Conservatives under Kemi Badenoch both welcomed Trump’s re-election in 2024, and Reform in particular has built its identity in part on closeness to the US president. Both parties have increasingly tried to dissociate themselves from him amid questions over his temperament and judgement, and his attacks on Iran have led to rising global energy prices, a pressure felt directly by British households.",
        "Trump’s warmth towards Putin comes amid a renewed US effort to end the Ukraine war by mediating talks, even as Russian strikes continue to pound Ukraine more than four years after the full-scale invasion. The contrast between the mediation effort and the public embrace of the Russian leader is precisely what drew the criticism: a photograph and two words, “my friend”, that, in London, read as an endorsement of a man Britain’s political class, left and right, calls a dictator and a butcher.",
        "For Farage, the episode is a test of balance. He has long presented his friendship with Trump as an asset, and as a channel through which Britain could influence Washington. Tice’s intervention suggests the party is now willing to criticise the president in public when it judges he has gone too far, and to do so in language as strong as anything the government itself has used.",
        "For the Conservatives, the calculation is simpler: distance from Trump costs them little and aligns them with the broad public consensus on Ukraine. For Reform, it is more delicate, because the party’s supporters are among the most sympathetic in Britain to the US president. That Tice was willing to call the post “horrific” and a “serious mistake” suggests the party believes the cost of silence would be higher.",
        "What happens next depends on whether Farage himself raises the matter with Trump, and on whether the US mediation effort produces anything that changes the picture in Ukraine. For now, the photograph has done something unusual in British politics: it has united the government, the official opposition and the party closest to Trump in condemning the same post, on the same day, in almost the same words."
      ],
