      "slug": "displaced-darfur-families-face-volatile-winter-as-sudan-aid",
      "sections": [
        "war-conflict",
        "world"
      ],
      "section": "war-conflict",
      "headline": "Displaced Darfur families face volatile winter as Sudan aid cuts bite",
      "dek": "Sudan's displacement crisis could worsen with winter approaching.",
      "date": "November 22, 2026",
      "source": "Al Jazeera",
      "source_url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/11/22/displaced-from-darfur-sudan-families-face-volatile-winter-as-aid-cuts-loom",
      "body": [
        "When Fatima Mohamed Haroun was displaced from West Darfur at the start of Sudan’s war in 2023, she never imagined she would find herself caring for four of her grandchildren far from home. Now living in a crumbling mudbrick building in al-Ghadir, north of Omdurman in Khartoum state, she has received no humanitarian assistance since her displacement, and with winter fast approaching, she has no blankets, no warm clothes and no heating for the children.",
        "“I cannot work or earn money, so we depend on neighbours who occasionally give us food. But even our neighbours sometimes have nothing to eat. When they have no food, my grandchildren and I go to bed hungry,” she told Al Jazeera. “We have no supplies to help us cope with the cold, not even blankets to keep us warm. We sleep on the ground with only the sky above us. The cold is severe, and the children are suffering. We appeal to the government and humanitarian organisations to provide food, blankets, covers and other basic necessities to help us endure the cold and protect our children.”",
        "Temperatures can drop significantly in Omdurman during the winter, and at night al-Ghadir can be particularly cold because of its location close to mountainous areas. Displaced families living in temporary shelters there are especially vulnerable because they lack blankets, winter clothing and heating facilities, and winter is expected to worsen conditions for families living in temporary shelters or unfinished buildings.",
        "Haroun is not alone. Kheira Matar Hassan, a displaced woman from Darfur, supports her seven children by washing and ironing clothes in the same area. As of July 30, Khartoum state was hosting 414,252 internally displaced people, according to data from the International Organization for Migration’s Displacement Tracking Matrix. Sudan has more than 8.5 million internally displaced people in total, according to the same tracking system.",
        "The war in Sudan, which broke out between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces paramilitary in April 2023, has led to one of the world’s worst humanitarian crises. Khartoum state, initially one of the war’s epicentres but now relatively more stable, still hosts large numbers of people who fled southern and western Sudan, where fighting continues. Millions have fled Darfur and other war zones to other parts of Sudan or neighbouring countries.",
        "The crisis is being made worse by cuts to food aid. The General Coordination for Displaced People and Refugees now warns that reduced food assistance could force families to skip meals and sell their remaining possessions to survive. Its spokesperson, Adam Rijal, told Radio Dabanga that funding shortages and cuts to World Food Program assistance could deprive displaced families of essential meals and force them to adopt increasingly desperate coping strategies, including withdrawing children from school, selling their remaining possessions, and reducing the number and size of meals.",
        "Children, women, older people and people with disabilities face particular risks of malnutrition and disease, Rijal said, calling on donors and international institutions to close the funding gap and ensure food assistance reaches those most in need. In a statement after an eight-day visit to Sudan last month, the World Food Program’s acting executive director, Carl Skau, said the agency has the capacity to reach millions of people caught between war, displacement and worsening drought, but is running out of money.",
        "At the same time, harvests are failing. Residents of Sheairia locality in East Darfur report a near-total failure of the agricultural season, sharply higher food prices, and shortages of medicines for people and livestock. Resident Babiker Ishaq told Radio Dabanga that farmers had harvested almost nothing of their main crops, including groundnuts, millet, sorghum and okra. A sack of millet costs about 350,000 Sudanese pounds when paid for through bank transfers, a sack of sorghum a similar amount, a pound of onions around 4,500 pounds and a bottle of cooking oil between 13,000 and 14,000 pounds. Deductions on transfers through the Bankak app, which Ishaq estimated at 40% in some cases and 50% in others, further reduce the money available for essential purchases. A rabies treatment injection costs about 300,000 pounds, he said, putting it beyond the reach of many families.",
        "The United Nations’ latest hunger assessment makes the outlook bleaker still. The Food and Agriculture Organization and the World Food Program identified a risk of famine in 13 areas and settlements for displaced people in North Darfur and South Kordofan if fighting intensifies and access to aid deteriorates, and found that nearly 19.5 million people, 41% of Sudan’s population, faced crisis levels of hunger or worse, with five million at emergency levels and more than 825,000 children projected to suffer severe acute malnutrition in 2026 without immediate action. Food and nutrition assistance to the five countries of greatest concern fell by 62% between 2022 and 2025.",
        "Drought linked to the El Niño climate pattern is adding to the strain, affecting up to 55% of cropland in Sennar state and 70% in Gedaref, two of Sudan’s main farming states. The World Food Program has forecast that drought could push an additional 350,000 people into severe hunger by the end of 2026. For Fatima Mohamed Haroun and her grandchildren, sleeping on the ground in al-Ghadir, those statistics are already reality."
      ],
      "related": [],
