      "section": "us",
      "headline": "Lock of former US President George Washington's hair sells for $4,000",
      "dek": "The hair was given as a sign of friendship shortly before Washington's death in 1799, according to a descendent of its former owner.",
      "date": "November 21, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwe9k23dpz0do",
      "body": [
        "A framed lock of hair belonging to George Washington, the first President of the United States, has sold for $4,000 at an auction in the United States, its history traced to a hotelier who hosted the founding father shortly before his death in 1799.",
        "The sale took place on Saturday, November 21, at Richmond Auctions, a South Carolina-based auction house. The identity of the buyer was not disclosed.",
        "The item itself is modest: a lock of hair set in a round, gold-gilded wooden frame measuring nine inches by two and a half inches, with a glass window. What gives it value is not the hair but the paper trail behind it, a chain of ownership stretching back more than two centuries to the last year of Washington's life.",
        "According to the auction house, the hair once belonged to John Gadsby, a resident of what in 1799 was the town of Alexandria in Virginia. Gadsby was a hotelier who ran the City Hotel and Tavern in Alexandria, and he entertained Washington for dinner shortly before the former president's death later that year. The account comes from a testament given in 1928 by Gadsby's granddaughter, Julia Ten Eyck McBlair, which was posted on the Richmond Auctions website.",
        "Gadsby's connection to Washington went beyond a single dinner. Richmond Auctions states that he hosted Washington's two birthday celebrations, in 1798 and 1799, at his hotel, a mark of the standing he held in Alexandria society and of the friendship between the two men. The lock of hair was given as a sign of that friendship, and it passed down through the Gadsby family before being obtained by the collector John K. Lattimer from one of Gadsby's descendants.",
        "That kind of gift was not unusual in its time. In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, a lock of hair was a common keepsake between friends and family, given as a sign of affection, and kept after death as a memorial. Hair does not decay the way other relics do, which is why so many locks from the period survive. Dozens of locks of Washington's hair are known to exist today, cut at different moments of his life and given to different people, each with its own chain of custody.",
        "The market for such relics is small but serious. Saturday's sale, at $4,000, was not the most expensive for Washington's hair at auction: other locks have previously commanded prices nearing $40,000. The difference lies in provenance, condition and the story attached, and in this case, the Gadsby connection, with its direct link to Washington's final year, was the selling point.",
        "The lock is one of dozens known to have survived for more than two centuries, according to reports of the sale, a reminder of how widely Washington's hair was distributed as keepsakes in his lifetime and after his death. Other locks have sold for far more: previous auctions have seen Washington's hair command prices nearing $40,000, alongside other treasured memorabilia from the founding era.",
        "For Richmond Auctions, the lot was one of those objects that auction houses prize most: small in itself, but carrying a story that reaches back to the founding of the republic. A lock of hair, a gold frame, a hotelier in Alexandria, two birthday dinners and a granddaughter's testament from 1928, together they make a $4,000 object into a piece of American history. The buyer, whoever they are, has bought more than a framed curiosity: a direct, documented link to the last year of the first president's life, and to the Alexandria hotelier who counted him a friend.",
        "Sources: BBC, Richmond Auctions and the Christian Post."
      ],
