      "section": "world",
      "headline": "Fight over fossil fuels leaves COP31 climate talks in deadlock",
      "dek": "Countries from nearly 200 countries are in a deadlock to try and get a new global climate agreement.",
      "date": "November 21, 2026",
      "source": "BBC",
      "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6ly5w40eg07o",
      "body": [
        "The COP31 climate talks in Antalya, Turkey, have fallen into deadlock over fossil fuels, pushing the UN summit into overtime as nearly 200 countries struggle to reach an agreement on the future of global climate action.",
        "The conference was due to end on Friday, but negotiations stretched into the weekend with no deal in sight. At the center of the impasse is the very cause of climate change itself: whether the final agreement will say anything at all about moving away from coal, oil and gas.",
        "The first draft of the summit's cover decision, the overarching agreement that sets out what the conference has achieved, was published on Monday and called for “the immediate transition away from fossil fuels in energy systems”. But all mentions of fossil fuels were stripped out of every subsequent draft, despite a push by more than 100 countries to keep them in.",
        "The countries blocking any reference to fossil fuels are the world's petrostates, including Saudi Arabia and Russia, which have long opposed the inclusion of any language on phasing them out. Their position has hardened this year: with the war between the United States and Iran roiling the Middle East and causing the world's largest energy supply shock, oil producers have argued for carbon capture technology instead of a phase-out, a technology critics describe as costly and ineffective.",
        "The talks are being led by Australia, which is chairing the negotiations, and hosted by Turkey, whose environment minister Murat Kurum holds the COP31 presidency. Under the UN's consensus rules, just one of the nearly 200 nations involved can block agreement, which gives the blocking countries enormous leverage, and leaves the rest of the room waiting on them.",
        "The deadlock comes at a critical time for the planet. While attention has been diverted by geopolitical turbulence, the climate crisis is accelerating after a year of climate-linked disasters from Europe to Asia. For the countries most vulnerable to rising seas and extreme weather, a deal that does not even mention fossil fuels would be worse than no deal at all, and several, including the European Union, Panama, the Marshall Islands and Norway, have said they will not endorse one.",
        "The dispute is also about money. Developing countries need funds to transition their energy systems and to respond to natural disasters, but the talks have made little progress on climate finance. Under the 2015 Paris Agreement, developed countries are required to provide financial support to developing nations, yet wars and strained budgets have left wealthy nations unwilling to commit the trillions of dollars involved.",
        "There is history hanging over Antalya. At COP28 in Dubai in 2023, nations agreed for the first time to transition away from fossil fuels in energy systems, a commitment widely described as a landmark. COP31 was supposed to build on that agreement, renewing the commitment to triple renewable energy capacity and double energy efficiency improvements by 2030. Instead, even echoing those agreed outcomes has proved politically difficult for some countries with strong vested interests in fossil fuels.",
        "The United States, notably, is not part of the argument at all. Under President Donald Trump, Washington has pulled out of international climate action, leaving the talks to proceed without the world's second-largest emitter at the table.",
        "As the summit heads into overtime, the question is whether the presidency can find wording that bridges the divide, or whether COP31 becomes the first UN climate conference since Copenhagen in 2009 to end without an agreed deal. Either way, the deadlock has already laid bare the enormous gap between those seeking ambitious action and the oil-rich nations pushing back.",
        "Sources: CNN, Al Jazeera and AFP reporting from COP31."
      ],
