      "headline": "Three French police officers sentenced over racist assault on Black man",
      "dek": "Paris criminal court finds racism was an aggravating factor in the 2020 assault of Black music producer Michel Zecler.",
      "date": "November 21, 2026",
      "source": "Al Jazeera",
      "source_url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/21/three-french-police-officers-sentenced-over-racist-assault-on-black-man",
      "body": [
        "A French court has sentenced three police officers to prison for the racially motivated beating of Black music producer Michel Zecler, in a landmark ruling that recognised the racist nature of the violence and could have implications for how police misconduct and racial discrimination are addressed in France. The Paris criminal court delivered its verdict late on Friday, November 20, almost six years to the day after the assault.",
        "The officers, 29-year-old Aurélien Lapersonne, 50-year-old Philippe Tondeux and 37-year-old Pierre Plantier, were each sentenced to five years in prison, with varying portions suspended. Lapersonne, who was found to have first stopped Zecler and authored the falsified police report, had three years of his sentence suspended. Tondeux had three and a half years suspended. Plantier had four years suspended and will serve the remaining year under electronic monitoring, with an ankle tag, rather than in prison. Lapersonne and Tondeux were to be jailed at a later date, under deferred committal orders.",
        "In addition to the prison sentences, the court issued permanent bans on all three men serving as police officers, effective immediately, even in the event of an appeal. The sentences were significantly lower than those sought by the prosecutor, who had requested four to six years in prison, but the court’s recognition of racism as an aggravating factor was, for campaigners, the more important part of the ruling.",
        "The court found the three men guilty of aggravated group assault with the use of weapons, which left Zecler unable to work for more than eight days, in his case, 45 days, and of falsifying an official report. The presiding judge told them they had drafted and signed an arrest statement they knew to be inaccurate, in order to shield themselves from disciplinary and legal consequences. All three had admitted to having used excessive force and apologised to Zecler, though they denied the charges of racism.",
        "Most notably, while the officers were not initially charged with racially motivated violence, the judges ultimately made the significant decision to recognize the aggravating circumstance. The court found that Lapersonne did in fact utter a racial slur during the assault, and determined that the racist element extended to the other two officers involved, even though they themselves had not used racist language. “What is very important is that the racist nature of the violence he suffered has been recognized,” Zecler’s lawyer, Serge Money, told France Info, hailing a “historic ruling”.",
        "The assault took place on November 21, 2020, during France’s second Covid-19 lockdown. Officers stopped Zecler outside his Paris music studio, accusing him of failing to wear a compulsory face mask and suspecting him of cannabis use, and of grabbing one of their weapons. The beating, which was caught on camera, left Zecler with injuries that kept him from work for 45 days. He told the Associated Press after the incident that the officers hurled repeated insults at him, including a very strong racist epithet.",
        "The case triggered renewed outrage in France over police violence, which victims and researchers say is systemic but which authorities and law enforcement unions claim is limited to isolated cases. Campaigners and rights groups saw the case as further evidence of what they describe as systemic racism in the French police, in a country where they say ethnic minorities receive far harsher treatment than white people. The trial, which ran for two weeks from November 9, was packed on every day, and Zecler emerged from the courtroom on the final day to applause and cheers from his supporters.",
        "The verdict was welcomed by Zecler’s lawyer and by anti-racism campaigners, but it also underlined how rare such an outcome is. Convictions of police officers for racist violence are uncommon in France, and the recognition of racism as an aggravating factor, rather than as a separate charge, was a judicial choice that could set a precedent for future cases. “Ultimately, they were convicted of lying, as the falsification of written records was established,” Money said.",
        "The officers’ permanent bans from the police force take effect immediately, and their prison terms, though partly suspended, mean that all three will serve time. For Zecler, now 47, the verdict closes a six-year wait for justice, but the applause in the courtroom was for more than one man’s case. It was for a court that, for once, had said plainly what the video had shown all along.",
        "What happens next will be watched closely. If the officers appeal, the bans and the sentences will be tested again. But the ruling stands as a marker: a French court has found that police violence can be racist, that falsifying a report to cover it up is a crime in itself, and that the officers responsible can be sent to prison, and barred from the force for good."
      ],
