      "section": "europe",
      "headline": "Better educated than ever, yet Germany's Gen Z is being shut out of its own future",
      "dek": "Never before have young people faced so many challenges. Young Germans are asking: Will I be better off later in life than my parents are now?",
      "date": "November 20, 2026",
      "source": "DW",
      "source_url": "https://www.dw.com/en/better-educated-than-ever-yet-germany-s-gen-z-is-being-shut-out-of-its-own-future/a-80005823",
      "body": [
        "Young Germans are better educated than any generation before them, and yet many of them feel shut out of the future they were promised. That contradiction brought thousands of them onto the streets on November 20, World Children’s Day, in 73 towns and cities across Germany, in protests that asked a simple question: will we be better off than our parents?",
        "The demonstrations were part of a wider wave of youth protests across Europe that day, organized in Germany under the banner Gen Z regelt, Generation Z takes charge. The young people taking part were not only protesting about one policy or one party. Their message, repeated in placards and speeches, was that education no longer guarantees what it used to: a secure job, an affordable home, and a say in the decisions that will shape their lives.",
        "The frustration has a factual basis, according to a major new United Nations report. The United Nations Population Fund’s 2026 World Population Report, entitled Succeeding Generations: Empowering Young People to Chart the Future, is based on a survey of 109,000 people in 73 countries. It found that young people worldwide are better educated than any generation before them, with 78 percent of adolescents now completing at least secondary education. Yet finding work is becoming harder, and informal, insecure employment is growing.",
        "The report also describes how economic uncertainty is pushing the milestones of adult life later and later. Financial independence, it found, often comes only in the second half of a person’s twenties, while stable partnerships are typically formed in the early to mid-thirties and parenthood only in the mid to late thirties. For young Germans, that delay is felt most sharply in two places: the job market and the housing market.",
        "Finding a job after university or vocational training has become noticeably harder, even for graduates with good qualifications. And finding an affordable place to live in a big city, the first step towards living independently, is, in the words of the report’s authors, becoming almost impossible. Rents in German cities have risen far faster than the starting salaries on offer to young workers, and the gap between the two has become one of the defining pressures on the generation.",
        "The second grievance is political. Although nearly half of the world’s population is under 30, people under 30 hold just 2.6 percent of parliamentary seats worldwide, according to the UN report. Erik Sczygiol, an 18-year-old who was until recently deputy secretary-general of Germany’s Federal Conference of Student Representatives, was one of the young people invited to the report’s launch in Bonn. He told Deutsche Welle that young people are often invited into political discussions only after legislation has already passed through numerous drafts, leaving little room for their proposals to change anything.",
        "“Young people are taking to the streets because they feel they are not being heard,” Sczygiol said. “And, of course, that feeling is strongly reinforced when the press devotes no more than a brief paragraph to their demands while focusing the rest of its coverage on how these protests could become problematic.” Florian Brod, 25, a Youth Delegate for Sustainable Development who represents young Germans at United Nations conferences, made a similar point: the generation that will live longest with today’s decisions has the least say in making them.",
        "What particularly infuriates many of the young protesters is the way they are talked about rather than spoken to. The youth wing of the conservative CDU/CSU alliance in the Berlin district of Mitte suggested deploying the German army against demonstrations. Bild, Germany’s highest-circulation tabloid, ran a headline that translated as “Young, male, known to the police, who rioted after the demonstrations and where chaos threatens today.” For the protesters, that kind of coverage is the problem in miniature: their demands get a paragraph, while the risk of disorder gets the front page.",
        "Climate policy is another sore point. Young people say they see little action from politicians on the issues that matter most to them, and the climate conference in Antalya, Turkey, was cited by the protesters as a case in point. The conference, known as COP31, has been dominated by arguments over climate science and finance, while the young people who will live with the consequences watch from the sidelines.",
        "The protests on November 20 were not a one-off. They were the latest in a series of youth-led actions across Europe this autumn, and the organizers say they will continue. Their demands are not radical in themselves: jobs that match their qualifications, homes they can afford, action on the climate, and a genuine voice in politics. What makes the moment striking is the gap between what this generation has achieved, the best education in history, and what it is being offered in return.",
        "For Germany’s political parties, the message is uncomfortable. A generation that did everything it was asked to do, stay in school, get the qualifications, play by the rules, is now asking, in public and in large numbers, whether the bargain still holds. The answer they get in the coming months, on jobs, housing and climate, will decide whether the protests of November were a warning or the start of something bigger.",
        "Sources: Deutsche Welle, the United Nations Population Fund’s 2026 World Population Report, and reporting on the November 20 protests."
      ],
